\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-Japan's MOF warns of  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  action, others reserved.  \b0\par\par\uc2 366 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 08:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 24 (Reuters) - A senior Japanese Finance Ministry official warned on Wednesday that the dollar's exchange rate was out of line with economic fundamentals, words officials have used in the past before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market, but others in Tokyo were more ambivalent about the need to take action.   \par \par \uc2  "Looking at (recent) economic indicators, I strongly feel there is no need for such a one-sided selling of the dollar," the official told Reuters, indicating that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  had been monitoring the dollar's fall closely.   \par \par \uc2  "I think recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements are rapid and not reflecting economic fundamentals," he added.   \par \par \uc2  He was speaking as the dollar hovered around 103.45 yen , just above a four-year low of 102.70 hit on Friday. It hit a record low of $1.3118 per euro on Wednesday afternoon.   \par \par \uc2  However, the head of a major Japanese business group said that while he was worried exporters could suffer if the dollar kept on falling, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should be determined by market forces rather than government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "What we are seeing now is dollar weakness, rather than yen strength," said Kakutaro Kitashiro, chairman of the Japan Association of Corporate Executives (Keizai Doyukai).   \par \par \uc2  "If other countries were to jointly \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would be another matter, but I don't see the need for (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) now."   \par \par \uc2  A senior Japanese ruling party official also voiced doubts about the effectiveness of market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the longer term.   \par \par \uc2  "Considering the amount of trade that goes on, the impact of any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be temporary and limited," Kaoru Yosano, policy chief of the Liberal Democratic Party, told a news conference.   \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is meant for avoiding abnormal \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements prompted by temporary capital flows and not for deciding the ultimate \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels," he added.   \par \par \uc2  Tokyo sold a massive 35 trillion yen ($339 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in 2003 and the first three months of 2004 in an attempt to shore up the dollar and limit the impact of its weakness on Japanese exporters, but it does not appear to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the latest dollar weakness, traders said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041124e0bo006bt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
