\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Dollar near euro lows, Asian stocks gain.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Kaufman  \par \uc2 759 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 04:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 24 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near a record low around $1.31 per euro on Wednesday, but Asian stocks rose as investors turned their attention away from exporters to pick up companies less exposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings.  \par \par \uc2 News that Russia may consider boosting the share of euros in its $100 billion-plus \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves and a rebound in oil prices that is clouding the U.S. economic outlook helped to undermine the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Although there has been talk about Russia's foreign reserves for a while, the news was used as an excuse to sell the dollar," said Kikuko Takeda, market economist at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 "The rise in oil prices also didn't help the dollar in the near-term."  \par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei average gained 0.39 percent in morning trade as bargain hunting in plays on the domestic economy outweighed selling of exporters such as Canon Inc. (7751.T).  \par \par \uc2 An MSCI index of non-Japanese Asian shares rose 0.44 percent to near its highest levels in almost 5 years.  \par \par \uc2 Japan Tobacco Inc. (JT) (2914.T) jumped 3.29 percent, Mitsui O.S.K. Lines (9104.T), Japan's number two ocean freight firm, climbed 4.41 percent and Mitsubishi Tokyo Financial Group (MTFG) (8306.T) rose 1.36 percent.  \par \par \uc2 Australian stocks touched a record high, supported by gains in BHP Billiton (BHP.AX). The benchmark S&P/ASX 200 index climbed 0.28 percent to 3,877.1 by 0235 GMT, after hitting an all-time high of 3,889.8.  \par \par \uc2 BHP Billiton put on 1.1 percent, extending Tuesday's gains after it completed a bigger-than-expected A$2.3 billion ($1.8 billion) buyback of 4.8 percent of its Australian-listed shares.  \par \par \uc2 Most other Asian share markets were higher, led by a 1.07 percent gain on South Korea's KOSPI , but Hong Kong inched off a 3-1/2-year closing high.  \par \par \uc2 GOLD UP, OIL DOWN, DOLLAR REELING  \par \par \uc2 Gold was steady after hitting a 16-1/4-year high in London at $449.50 an ounce. Spot gold was bid at $447.40 versus $447.55 in late U.S. trade.  \par \par \uc2 NYMEX crude oil prices edged lower after giving up much of their gains from Tuesday's session and traders expected more profit-taking ahead of the U.S. Thanksgiving holidays and amid mild weather in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. light crude for January delivery was down 11 cents at $48.83 a barrel. It peaked for the day at $50.25 in U.S. trade, up $1.62 to its highest since Nov. 4.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar, reeling from a market view that Washington may want the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall to help cap the huge U.S. current account deficit, hit a 9-year trough versus the Swiss franc and a 4-month low versus the British pound after Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the bank was reviewing its \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves.  \par \par \uc2 Its euro holdings have risen to roughly 25-30 percent compared with 10 percent 2 years ago.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bought around $1.3046 per euro , little changed from levels in late U.S. trade and a stone's throw from the record low of $1.3108.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was little changed at around 103.45 yen , off a 4-year low of 102.70 hit on Friday and barely reacting to slightly better-than-expected service industries data in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 WARY OF \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tertiary sector index rose 0.1 percent in September, beating forecasts for a fall of 0.1 percent, but traders were hesitant about buying the yen, wary that its rise could prompt Japanese authorities to restart dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Tokyo has sold a massive 35 trillion yen ($338.6 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the past 2 years, although it has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-March.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was unchanged at around 1.1574 Swiss francs , near the 9-year low of 1.1562. Sterling bought around $1.8680 , not far from the 4-month high of $1.8729.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond prices retreated from multi-month highs hit earlier this week. With a Japanese holiday on Tuesday and a holiday in the United States on Thursday, investors were focusing less on trading and more on position adjustments.  \par \par \uc2 The key 10-year JGB yield was up 1.5 basis point at 1.415 percent, after hovering near a 2-month low of 1.4 percent on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041124e0bo00335\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
