\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Dollar Drops In World Of Wishful Thinking  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Laurence Norman  \par \uc2 988 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2004\par 08:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--If a lesson can be drawn from the past few days, it's that the policy makers most influential in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets seem to be resorting to wishful thinking rather than any coherent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies to influence exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 If policy and reality become increasingly detached, the credibility of these officials may be increasingly undermined. That's important given growing concerns that dollar declines could at some point become a dollar rout, with unwelcome effects for U.S. markets and the global economy.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. has the honor of being chief dreamer, since its dollar policy seems to rest on two divergent goals - one for the short-term and another very different one for the longer-term.  \par \par \uc2 The professed policy, as U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow must grimly keep repeating, is for a 'strong dollar.' Until now, this has been backed by no policy tools: no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , no attempts to talk up the dollar and no action that has made anyone believe that either the budget deficit or the current account deficit will be cut back significantly in the near future.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants widely believe the real U.S. hope is for a steady dollar decline, at least in the shorter-term, with the apparent policy being one of benign neglect.  \par \par \uc2 That policy exists even as senior U.S. officials continue to argue otherwise. As happened at last weekend's meetings of the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation in Chile and the Group of 20 developed and industrialized nations in Berlin, the Bush administration continues to talk about a strong dollar policy and promises its main trading partners it will implement the fiscally- disciplined policies that would build the basis for a strong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Nor has the European Union played a very coherent tune. After initially appearing calm about dollar weakness, the tone switched dramatically a couple of weeks back, when top officials from France, Germany and Italy began to ring the alarm bells about euro strength. They were soon followed by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet, who described the euro appreciation as 'brutal' and the verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - something known in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets as jawboning - has been in full swing since then.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     Hollow Words \par  \par   Yet such attempts to push the euro down have amounted to little so far and the euro has been testing new ground on an almost daily basis. After weeks of failing to back the words with action - namely \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  - investors are increasingly brushing off comments by European officials. \par   That's all the more so, since European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would only be truly effective if it comes in concert with other global policymakers, in particular the U.S. Since Snow and Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan disparaged the very idea of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in speeches last week, analysts believe euro zone policymakers have little choice but to wait, moan and hope. \par   'The bottom line is action speaks louder than words. You can jawbone all you want ... but you've got to back it up with action,' said Simon Fowles, chief trader at Wells Fargo in San Francisco. \par   If any of the Big Three economic areas come close to having a recognizable policy, it's Japan. Vice Finance Minister Hiroshi Watanabe and Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki have been painstakingly consistent in their comments over the past month, gradually hiking their rhetoric about whether the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is considering \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the markets. And after the $140 billion the BoJ spent on behalf of Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the first quarter to stem the yen's rise, few in the market are blase about the prospect of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , even if it's unilateral. \par   Yet even that is centered around an event Japan can't control: China's eventual move to a flexible exchange rate. Many in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets believe China is planning to announce some sort of change to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime, which currently confines the Chinese yuan to a tightly-controlled band against the dollar. \par   If China were to allow the yuan to appreciate, it would make it more difficult for Japan to argue that it needs to weaken the value of the yen as it seeks to maintain export competitiveness amid a nascent economic recovery. It would also likely spark the ire of the U.S. \par   If China doesn't revalue the yuan in the coming months, a slide in the dollar could start to squeeze Japanese exporters in 2005 and that would make it difficult for Japan to stay out of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. \par  \par    The Dangers Of Discord \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 All this, said David Gilmore, partner at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics, reveals a global financial community whose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy mix is increasingly 'dysfunctional.'  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 'The message that came out of the G20 meeting, and to a lesser extent the APEC meeting in Santiago, is that the major economies couldn't be further apart regarding the dollar right now,' Gilmore said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The last time this happened on a major scale was in 1987, he noted, when the U.S. administration argued with West Germany over monetary policy and turned to a weaker dollar policy that contributed to the 1987 stock market crash.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Major disagreements over \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy 'either begin a trend or exacerbate a current trend. Signs of disharmony or discord are not helpful and should exacerbate market selling.'  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the dollar hitting a fresh all-time low against the euro and near its four-year low against the yen Tuesday, the danger of history repeating itself shouldn't be brushed off.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He has previously covered the Argentine economy for Dow Jones in Buenos Aires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 1-201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041124e0bo0040i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
