\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits new lows as bearish sentiment persists.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 657 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2004\par 16:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, adds comment and quotes, updates prices, changes byline and dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 23 (Reuters) - The dollar slumped on Tuesday after news that Russia may increase the share of euros in its reserves drove the European unit to a new lifetime high.  \par \par \uc2 Confirmation of last week's talk Russia might make such a move triggered another bout of dollar selling, underscoring the depth of bearish sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Already under heavy selling pressure from dealers concerned the U.S. is struggling to fund its record trade and current account deficits, the greenback buckled again on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The main thing was the report from Russia that they're going to boost their euro (holdings) ... but it seems more of a market-driven move than any fresh news," said Nick Bennenbroek, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Brown Brothers Harriman in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Alexei Ulyukayev, First Deputy Chairman at Russia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , told a news conference: "It is unlikely that the dollar share of reserves will be reviewed. The share of euros may be reviewed, but first we need to undertake analysis."  \par \par \uc2 Last week, Deputy Chairman Konstantin Korishchenko said the proportion of the euro in the basket that helps guide the value of the rouble was bigger than many analysts expected.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers used the latest comments to try to take out euro options barriers at $1.3100 but the move ran out of steam, and with everyone seemingly on the same side of the trade the euro's ascent may be a slow one.  \par \par \uc2 "With so much money already positioned for a drop in the dollar, these gains (in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) are being very hard fought," Bennenbroek at BBH said.  \par \par \uc2 Mid-morning in New York, the euro was up 0.3 percent at $1.3077 compared with late on Monday. Earlier Tuesday, it climbed as high as $1.3093, according to Reuters data.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was around 0.5 percent stronger at $1.8697, as was the Australian dollar, up at $0.7865.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker at 1.1588 Swiss francs. Earlier in the session, it fell to a new 9-year low of around 1.1567 francs.  \par \par \uc2 On a flows-weighted basis, the dollar was also down at 82.93. It had traded at a 9-year low of 82.81.  \par \par \uc2 Weakness against the yen was less pronounced due to resilience in the euro/yen and market wariness about possible dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar's on a downward path. The real test the market is going to face is \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said David Bloom, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in London.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was more or less flat at 103.17 yen.  \par \par \uc2 GREENSPAN FACTOR  \par \par \uc2 In other asset markets, gold hit a new 16-year high just shy of $450 and ounce, while crude oil futures in New York were up around 0.5 percent at $48.84 a barrel.  \par \par \uc2 Treasuries were down ahead of a $24 billion auction of new two-year notes, with the yield on the 10-year note at around 4.2 percent.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks opened slightly lower.  \par \par \uc2 Comments last week from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan that reinforced the market view Washington wants a weaker dollar are still reverberating around \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 A weekend meeting of Group of 20 finance officials offered no strong measures to halt the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline.  \par \par \uc2 The G20 communique did not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility explicitly, a phrase which could have stayed dollar bears' hands for a while in anticipation of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, although analysts said fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had heightened.  \par \par \uc2 "But any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  we do see will more likely be aimed at smoothing the dollar decline rather than halting or reversing it," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Royal Bank of Scotland. (Additional reporting by Katie Hunt in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041123e0bn00ao7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
