\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar recovers slightly in holiday trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 524 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2004\par 09:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 23 (Reuters) - The U.S. dollar moved further away from recent multi-year lows against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Tuesday, as dealers took profits on a Japanese holiday and ahead of a U.S. Thanksgiving holiday on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 After a weekend meeting of Group of 20 finance officials offered no strong measures to halt the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline, the market was pausing to see where it would go next.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar found some respite from last week's record lows against the euro, 4-1/2 year lows against the yen and near-9-year lows against the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 "I won't be suprised if euro/dollar heads higher in coming weeks but for now we've come off," said Mitul Kotecha, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Calyon.  \par \par \uc2 "It's a holiday-shortened week in the U.S. and Japan's on holiday today. It's adjusting positions rather than any fundamental news."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at $1.30 per euro at 0830 GMT, up a third of a percent from the U.S. close. It was at 103.40 yen, up slightly from New York closing levels.  \par \par \uc2 GREENSPAN FACTOR  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's two-month long decline took it to a record low of $1.3074 per euro and a trough near 102.75 against the yen last week.  \par \par \uc2 The fall, driven by a belief the United States wants to see the dollar weaken to ease its massive current account deficit, gathered momentum on Friday after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan said the current account gap was unsustainable.  \par \par \uc2 The view strengthened when the G20 communique failed to explicitly mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility, although analysts said fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, particularly by Asian central banks and the Bank of Japan, have heightened this week.  \par \par \uc2 Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were below their recent multi-year highs while the Canadian dollar dropped to about C$1.1892/95, from 12-year highs set on Monday of C$1.1818.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said markets were probably surprised the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  yet.  \par \par \uc2 "If you look at the euro, yen and the Aussie, those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are approaching levels which could potentially trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Callum Henderson, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Standard Chartered Bank in Singapore, citing suspected Reserve Bank of Australia \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the Australian dollar struck 80 U.S. cents in February.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was trading just above US$0.78.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said dollar-selling would resume after the U.S. Thanksgiving holiday if the fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  proved unfounded, given it is a light week for data in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 "There is good appetite to sell dollar/yen on rallies, and this is despite the threat of possible unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the BOJ. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks rise on a move toward 100," said John Kyriakopoulos, strategist at National Australia Bank in Singapore.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. home sales and the Chicago Fed National Activity Index for October are due at 1500 GMT, and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell speaks at 1940 GMT.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041123e0bn007c4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
