\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar teeters above record low versus euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 13:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Re-leads, updates price action, comment, changes byline)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The dollar slid back within half a cent of last week's record low against the euro on Monday after a weekend meeting of world finance chiefs ended with no agreement to stem the greenback's decline.  \par \par \uc2 G20 policymakers gathered in Berlin made no mention of the dollar's decline in a communique issued at the end of their meeting, reinforcing the view that the United States is happy to tolerate a weaker dollar to ease its trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was hovering above Friday's 4-1/2 year low against the yen, with a G20 call for more \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility in Asia expected to put more pressure on the greenback over time.  \par \par \uc2 "The background remains very dollar-negative but people are already very short of dollars and may be tempted to trim positions ahead of the Thanksgiving holiday (on Thursday)," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Bank.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was down slightly on the day at $1.3035 per euro  and little\par changed at 103.10 yen        by the European midsession.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar hit a record low of $1.3074 to the euro last Thursday after several weeks of broad-based selling pressure that also knocked it to a nine-year low against a basket of major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In the next few months it is difficult to see any dollar stabilisation and the G20 did not tell us anything new," said Hans-Guenter Redeker, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "The dollar may be correcting a bit because many speculative players are massively short the dollar."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 GOING IT ALONE?  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the hands-off approach from G20 policymakers left the dollar vulnerable, particularly after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan highlighted the unsustainable widening in the U.S. current account deficit on Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell below 103.00 yen for the first time in over four years on Friday in the wake of Greenspan's comments, and analysts said the threat of unilateral \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was the only thing stopping it falling further.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese policymakers have made clear they are unhappy with a stronger yen and recent figures suggest Japan's export-led recovery is already slowing.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since March, after a record 20 trillion yen ($193 billion) of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2003 but Japanese officials are upping the ante.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said in an interview published in the Financial Times newspaper that an appreciation of the yen could be destabilising and become a big concern.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "With the dollar trading below the bottom marked in March of 103.40, I'd say \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could take place any time," said Toshihiro Azuma, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manager at Sumitomo Trust and Banking Corp.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOLERANCE THRESHOLD  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts do not expect the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the euro's strength until the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reaches $1.35 or even $1.40, but politicians in the region are already talking tough.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said on Saturday the euro's rise was "worrying" and blamed the United States for not addressing its budget and current account deficits.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Following Schroeder's critique, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said his country was committed to cutting its record budget deficit in half over the next four years. But in a swipe back at Europe, he said all countries were responsible for boosting growth and correcting trade imbalances.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the biggest movers of the day was the Canadian dollar which rose within a whisker of 12-year highs against the greenback after bullish comments from the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking in Berlin, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said the recent rise in the Canadian dollar was "not inappropriate" and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should only be considered in "extraordinary circumstances".   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041123e0bm005lg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
