\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar dips against euro as G20 offers no reprieve.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 23:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped against the euro on Monday in thin trading after a weekend meeting of the Group of 20 finance ministers and central bankers in Berlin offered no concrete measures to stem the dollar's decline.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. markets will close on Thursday for the Thanksgiving holiday, meaning trading this week will be lighter than usual.  \par \par \uc2 The G20 communique did not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reinforcing the market's view that the United States is willing to tolerate a weaker dollar to ease its burgeoning current account deficit - broadly, a measure of the nation's global trade.  \par \par \uc2 This view gained credence last Friday when Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warned the current account gap was unsustainable and foreign demand for U.S. securities will likely wane.  \par \par \uc2 "The fundamental factor hurting the dollar was the lack of direction or the lack of concern that the G20 statement conveyed to the market about the the dollar's recent bout of weakness," said Joe Francomano, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "It seems that the benign neglect policy of (both) the United States ... and the global community about the dollar's weakness is still in place," he added.  \par \par \uc2 In late afternoon trade in New York, the euro was up 0.20 percent at $1.3043 compared with late Friday and close to its record high of $1.3074 reached last week.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts at IDEAglobal do not expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the euro's rise against the dollar, until the euro zone \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hits $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 "As long as bond yields don't rise or equity prices fall purely on dollar weakness, we very much doubt the U.S. administration will choose to apply the brakes to the current dollar drop," said IDEAglobal analysts in a research note.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was up slightly against the yen at 103.15 yen  but flat\par against the Swiss franc at 1.1625 francs       . Sterling rose to\par $1.8599       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders said the extent of the dollar's recent losses has forced market participants to book profits on the euro and take a pause in selling the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , though the dollar remained on the defensive and close to lows touched last week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market's taking a breather here ... but it still doesn't feel like the dollar's going to get much of a reprieve, even in a thin market," said Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corporation in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the G20's hands-off approach left the dollar vulnerable to further losses, especially against the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell below 103.00 yen for the first time in over four years after Greenspan's comments and analysts said only the threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stopped it falling more.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Recent statements from Japanese authorities suggesting that near-term moves of the dollar against the yen have not been driven by economic fundamentals underscored the growing risk of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , analysts said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the biggest movers of the day was the Canadian dollar which rose to a fresh 12-year high at C$1.1826 against the greenback, according to Reuters data, after bullish comments on the Canadian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking in Berlin, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said the recent rise in the Canadian dollar was "not inappropriate" and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should only be considered in "extraordinary circumstances". (Additional reporting by Jamie McGeever).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041122e0bm00i7l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
