\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar broadly flat as market takes breather.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 605 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 16:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates prices, adds quotes and comment, changes byline and dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The dollar was little changed on Monday as dealers paused from aggressively selling it and mulled the fallout from a weekend meeting in Berlin of Group of 20 finance ministers and central bankers.  \par \par \uc2 The G20 communique did not mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , reinforcing the market view that the U.S. is willing to tolerate a weaker dollar to ease its bulging trade and current account deficits.  \par \par \uc2 This view gained credence on Friday when Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warned the deficits were unsustainable and foreign demand for U.S. securities will likely wane.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar remains under heavy selling pressure, and on Monday hovered close to the lows it plumbed last week, but the extent of its recent losses forced many dealers to take profit, adjust positions and take a time out.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's taking a breather here," said Patrick Brodie, chief dealer at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking Corporation in New York. "That might have something to do with the holiday this week, but it still doesn't feel like the dollar's going to get much of a reprieve, even in a thin market."  \par \par \uc2 U.S. markets will close on Thursday for the Thanksgiving holiday, meaning trading this week will be lighter than usual.  \par \par \uc2 Mid-morning in New York, the euro was up 0.1 percent at $1.3033 compared with late Friday. This is within half a cent of its record high of $1.3074 reached last week.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar was flat at 103.03 yen  and 1.1622 Swiss francs       ,\par while sterling was a little weaker at $1.8547       .\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar index, which is weighted according to daily trading flows against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's main counterparts, was up marginally at 83.18 .  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  RISK STILL THERE  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the G20 policymakers' hands-off approach left the dollar vulnerable, particularly after Greenspan's speech.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell below 103.00 yen for the first time in over four years after his comments and analysts said only the threat of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was stopping it falling further.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese policymakers have made clear they are unhappy with a stronger yen and recent figures suggest Japan's export-led recovery is already slowing.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market since March, after a record 20 trillion yen ($193 billion) of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in 2003 and a further 15 trillion yen spent in the first quarter of this year, but Japanese officials are upping the ante.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui told the Financial Times that yen appreciation could be destabilizing and become a big concern.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Though we do not rule out the possibility of official action to smooth the dollar's decline below 103 yen, there is now increasing risks that officials will come in only when the dollar falls to 100 yen," a JP Morgan research note said Monday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Many analysts, however, do not expect the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb the euro's strength until it reaches $1.35 or even $1.40.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One of the biggest movers of the day was the Canadian dollar which rose to a fresh 12-year high against the greenback after bullish comments from the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speaking in Berlin, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said the recent rise in the Canadian dollar was "not inappropriate" and that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  should only be considered in "extraordinary circumstances".  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar fell to C$1.1859 early on Monday, according to Reuters data.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041122e0bm00a6s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
