\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Sinking dollar gets no lifeline from G20.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 688 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 14:03\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 22 (Reuters) - The dollar slid back towards last week's record low against the euro on Monday after a weekend meeting of world finance chiefs ended with no agreement to stem the greenback's decline.  \par \par \uc2 G20 policymakers made no mention of the dollar's slide in a communique issued at the end of their meeting in Berlin, reinforcing the view that major nations have accepted the need for a weaker dollar to correct the United States' trade gap.  \par \par \uc2 The G20's call for more Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility was seen putting more downward pressure on the dollar, but the threat of unilateral action from Japan kept the greenback above Friday's 4-1/2 year low against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "There appears to be an acceptance of a weaker dollar trend even if Europe and Japan are not happy with it," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered. "The Americans are probably only too happy to see the dollar depreciate."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was little changed at 103.10 yen at 1248 GMT, having fallen below 103 on Friday for the first time since April 2000. Against the euro, the dollar was weaker at $1.3040, moving back within half a cent of record lows hit last Thurday.  \par \par \uc2 "The background remains very dollar-negative but people are already very short of dollars and may be tempted to trim positions," said Ian Gunner, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Mellon Bank.  \par \par \uc2 GOING IT ALONE?  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the hands-off approach from G20 policymakers left the dollar vulnerable, particularly after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan highlighted the unsustainable nature of the U.S. current account deficit on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking at a banking conference in Frankfurt, Greenspan warned foreign appetite for U.S. assets would not increase for ever and hinted that the dollar was likely to bear the brunt of readjusting the deficit. "Greenspan's comments on Friday were very significant, but equally significant was the euro's inability to push to new highs against the dollar," said Gunner, noting that market positioning may be starting to slow the dollar's downward momentum.  \par \par \uc2 In the longer term, most analysts are forecasting further losses for the dollar and expect complaints from European and Japanese politicians and exporters to get louder.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has already made clear it is unhappy with the export-damaging strength of the yen and some traders say it may not be long before Japanese authorities step into the market to sell yen for dollars.  \par \par \uc2 Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui said in an interview published in the Financial Times newspaper that an appreciation of the yen could be destabilising and become a big concern.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily at the start of the year to prevent the yen's strength from derailing its tentative export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 "With the dollar trading below the bottom marked in March of 103.40, I'd say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could take place any time," said Toshihiro Azuma, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Sumitomo Trust and Banking Corp.  \par \par \uc2 TOLERANCE THRESHOLD  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts do not expect the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the euro's strength until the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaches $1.35 or even $1.40, but euro zone politicians are already talking tough.  \par \par \uc2 German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder said on Saturday the euro's rise was "worrying" and blamed the United States for not addressing its budget and current account deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Following Schroeder's critique, U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said his country was committed to cutting its record budget deficit in half over the next four years. But in a swipe back at Europe, he said all countries were responsible for boosting growth and correcting trade imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 One of the biggest movers of the day was the Canadian dollar which rose within a whisker of 12-year highs against the greenback after bullish comments from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief.  \par \par \uc2 Speaking in Berlin, Bank of Canada Governor David Dodge said the recent rise in the Canadian dollar was "not inappropriate" and that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  should only be considered in "extraordinary circumstances".   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041122e0bm008w1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
