\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: More Trouble For Markets   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,553 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 US SUMMARY: Stocks Hammered After Greenspan Warning   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA          10456.91  loss 115.64     dn 1.0% \par NASDAQ        2070.63   loss 33.65      dn 1.6% \par S&P 500       1170.34   loss 13.21      dn 1.1% \par Dow Future    10433.00  loss 45.00      dn 0.4% \par NASDAQ Future 1549.00   loss 9.00       dn 0.6% \par S&P Future    1167.25   loss 1.75       dn 0.4% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.20% gain 0.20 \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 Stocks fell sharply Friday, with the Dow Jones Industrials losing more than 115 points, as Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan sounded a warning over the nation's spiraling trade deficit. Crude oil futures rose more than $2 per barrel, further pressuring stocks. Prices of Treasurys tumbled.   \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: Greenspan's unusually frank assessment of the trade imbalance and its effect on the U.S. economy worried many investors. The Fed chairman said the economy was resilient thus far, but foreign investment could wane should the deficits continue to build and the U.S. dollar remain weak.   \par \par \uc2 "Certainly that has investors worried, though I'm not entirely sure why Greenspan chose to make a case out of this," said Lincoln Anderson, chief investment officer at LPL Financial Services in Boston. "The lower dollar will eventually force importers to raise prices, and that'll help cut the trade deficit. But nonetheless, it was unusual for him to speak out on it like that, and it's having an effect."   \par \par \uc2 Friday's trading also was pressured by oil prices, which rose in response to a U.S. government report that showed a drop in inventory for distillates such as heating oil - critical as winter approaches. A barrel of light crude was quoted at $48.44, up $2.22, on the New York Mercantile Exchange.   \par \par \uc2 Despite oil prices and Greenspan's assessment of the trade deficit, analysts said that the overall market remains strong, and that Friday's selloff amounted to profit-taking after weeks of strong gains.   \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys suffered a poor session Friday, in the wake of surprisingly candid remarks by Greenspan.   \par \par \uc2 Greenspan said "it seems persuasive that, given the size of the U.S. current account deficit, a diminished appetite for adding to dollar balances must occur at some point."   \par \par \uc2 "Greenspan's comments were pretty negative for Treasurys" since diminished demand for dollars means foreigners will eventually buy fewer Treasurys among other U.S. asset classes, said Brian Edmonds, head of U.S. Treasury trading at Banc of America Securities in New York.   \par \par \uc2 "The bluntness of his remarks moved the bond market," said Jim Cusser, portfolio manager at Waddell & Reed in Kansas City, who manages $1.5 billion of fixed income assets. But, after the initial Treasury market reaction, "on reflection Greenspan's words are more of a hope than a warning," said Cusser. "Higher rates in the future would reflect improving growth prospects" he added.   \par \par \uc2 ASIAN SUMMARY: Weak Dollar Besets Tokyo Stocks   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par USD-Yen             103.32       gain  0.08    up 0.1% \par AUD-USD             0.7817       loss  0.0023  dn 0.3% \par Nikkei 225          10787.54     loss  295.30  dn 2.7% \par Hang Seng           13701.79     loss  85.89   dn 0.6% \par JGB Yield           1.4000% down 0.0300 \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo stocks plunged more than 2% Monday morning and the U.S. dollar traded just above its four-and-half-year lows against the yen, following a dollar selloff Friday in New York on concerns over the swollen U.S. trade deficit.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Swollen trade deficits eventually could threaten the U.S. economy by souring foreign appetites to invest in the U.S, Greenspan warned.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. President George W. Bush reiterated Sunday that his administration will maintain a strong-dollar policy.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is starting to think about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to halt the yen's recent rapid rise against the dollar, Vice Finance Minister Hiroshi Watanabe said Sunday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chinese officials spent the weekend reassuring their western counterparts that they appreciate concern over China's pegged exchange rate against the dollar, but stressed that the move to a more flexible system will be gradual.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Crude futures were little changed in Asian trade Monday, holding onto sharp gains that came Friday amid a resurgence of jitters about a possible supply crunch over the coming winter months.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks Headed For Another Drubbing   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-USD    1.3025      loss  0.0015  dn 0.1% \par Stlg-USD    1.8564      loss  0.0020  dn 0.1% \par USD-Franc   1.1630      gain  0.0015  up 0.2% \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks face further pressure at the open, while prices of government debt may also start slightly lower with the euro.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: Stocks face another tough session as Greenspan's comments Friday and resurfacing fears of crude price hikes take their toll on the markets.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. spreadbettor IG Index is forecasting a 26-point fall on the FTSE to 4735 and is calling the DAX down 35 at 4100 and CAC down 25 at 3773.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks ended lower Friday, with pharmaceutical stocks leading the retreat after a U.S. Food and Drug Administration official raised concerns about five medications on the market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 JP Morgan strategist Rahul Shah commented that the longer-term trends are still positive for equities. He said valuations look well-supported and the outlook for earnings growth remains positive. "However, in the short term, a weaker dollar may send negative signals to investors," he cautioned.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Government debt prices in Europe are likely to fall Monday, though during the week hardly any net new supply is expected.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European government bonds charted a volatile upward path Friday, taking their direction from the euro versus the U.S. dollar.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Greenspan pressured gilts and pushed bunds briefly into negative territory, but both markets recovered after the initial downward spike.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Greenspan was much more hawkish about rates," said Anthony O'Brien, gilts analyst at Barclays in London.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asked if emerging market economies are prepared for a higher interest rate environment or if new crises are around the corner, Greenspan said: "Rising interest rates have been advertised so long and in so many places that everyone who hasn't hedged by now is losing money."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Otherwise, bond markets are still uneasy about the direction of the global economy, and that uncertainty is exaggerating Friday's moves, said Monique Wong, fixed income analyst at UBS in London.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Liquidity is not as good," Wong said, adding that the high bond prices in the euro zone aren't totally justified, even with the region's weak economic outlook.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Unless you believe (the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) actually will cut rates, these prices are very expensive," she said   \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : After Greenspan laid out candidly what the markets knew but hoped against, the dollar now faces some real trials ahead, and officials in the G-7 may have to bite the bullet and order \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar languished Friday at or close to long-term lows against several major rivals, as investors seized on remarks from various top international monetary officials as an excuse to sell the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CALENDAR: Monday, Nov 22: Euro zone foreign trade   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT                                                         Expected  Previous \par 0900  EU   Sep     Euro zone foreign trade                  3B        3B \par 1100  GER  Nov     Bundesbank monthly bulletin, Frankfurt \par 1330  CAN  Sep     Retail Trade                             +0.1%     +0.8% \par 1400  FRA          Weekly T-bill auction \par 1430  GER          German banking assoc BDB press \par                    conference, Frankfurt \par 1430  EU           ECB calls for main refi ops bids \par N/A   EU           EU foreign and defense ministers meet, \par                    Brussels \par N/A   US           Pres Bush visits Cartagena, Colombia \par N/A   US           No economic indicators scheduled \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 22-11-04 0600GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Absa (ASA.JO): 1H Earnings \par Consensus headline EPS (DJ, 3 analysts): ZAR3.63 (ZAR3.24)    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note: "They may surprise on the upside as the past year has been an excellent trading environment for retail banks," says one analyst whose estimate is slightly above the consensus forecast.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Cambridge Antibody Technology (CATG): FY Earnings \par Consensus pretax loss (DJ, 3 analysts): GBP37.6M (GBP41.8M loss)    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Note: Analysts are mainly focusing on CAT's court case with Abbott (ABT), which opens Mon, regarding disputed royalty payments on sales of arthritis antibody drug Humira. "It's rare" for such disputes to reach the costly trial stage without settlement, says Merrill Lynch, "so both sides must believe they have equally strong arguments." Analysts also expect continued tightening of the belt on expenses.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OTHER SCHEDULED EVENTS: \par  \par Roularta Media Group (ROU.BT): 3Q Earnings \par  \par Hellenic Duty Free (HDF.AT): 9-Mo Earnings \par  \par Intralot (INLOT.AT): 9M Earnings \par  \par Em.TV (EVA.XE): 3Q Earnings \par  \par Petroplus International (37693.AE): 3Q Earnings \par  \par Naspers (NPSN): 1H Earnings \par  \par Merchant Retail (MRT.LN): 1H Earnings \par  \par Nord Anglia Education (NAE.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Torotrak (TRK.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Workspace Group (WKP.LN): 1H Earnings \par  \par Braemar Seascope (BMS.LN): 1H Earnings \par  \par Care UK (CUK.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Fountains (FNT.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Hamworthy (HMY.LN): 1H Earnings \par  \par Innovation Group (TIG.LN): FY Earnings \par  \par Prelude Trust (PDT.LN): 1H Earnings \par  \par UBC Media (UBC.LN): 1H Earnings \par  \par KarstadtQuelle (KAR.XE): EGM \par  \par Banca Nazionale del Lavoro (BNL.MI): Capital Increase \par  \par Natra (NAT.MC): EGM \par  \par Alisida (ALYSK.AT): EGM \par  \par Deutsche Boerse, KfW (DB1.XE; KFW.YY): Investor Conference \par  \par Enel (EN): Dividend Payment \par  \par Mediobanca (MB.MI): Dividend Payment \par  \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 22-11-04 0600GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 1011   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041122e0bm00061\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
