\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: G20, APEC Leave Only Japan To Support Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,271 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 07:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par  \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar was steady Monday in Asia after a weekend of high-level comments on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but it looked set to resume its slide, as no one but Japan appears ready to support the wilting U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 After finance ministers met in Germany and national leaders gathered in Chile, markets are generally where they left off last week - but with an even stronger conviction that the dollar is headed for more multiyear lows, especially against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Japan offered its strongest threat yet to buy dollars, but the U.S. only reinforced the impression it's happy to see a gradual dollar fall. Europe put the onus on Washington to clean up its fiscal house and China appeared in no rush to let its yuan rise against the beleaguered greenback.  \par \par \uc2 The lack of agreement in Berlin among finance ministers of the Group of 20 rich and developing countries means the markets will likely continue forging their own solution to global imbalances - a revaluation of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The threat of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  point man - plus the market's need to digest a punishing fall for the dollar in the past few session - were buoying the dollar in Asian trade Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "Now is the proper time for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe told Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of the Berlin meeting of Group of 20 finance officials Sunday. "The movement in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last seven days has been rapid and erratic, meaning this is the proper time to think of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That is our basic principle."  \par \par \uc2 In seven trading sessions, the dollar slumped 3.7% against the yen, logging a four-and-a-half-year low of Y102.70 Friday after Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan addressed the market's big gnawing worry - that the giant U.S. current-account deficits mean overseas investors eventually will lose their ardor for dollar assets.  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe's warning helped stabilize the dollar - it was trading at Y103.29 around 0655 GMT. The euro, meanwhile, was at $1.3034, off last week's record high $1.3075.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Bank of Japan - if it comes - looks likely only to brake the dollar's fall briefly and offer juicier levels to sell the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Analysts and traders are looking for the dollar to test Y100 before long, a level it hasn't seen since November 1995, and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are climbing, with the Korean won at a seven-year high.  \par \par \uc2 "There's no change to the medium-term yen-bullish outlook," said Citigroup \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Tomoko Fujii.  \par \par \uc2 "The prospect of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation has been maintained," she said in a research report. "But since Y100 is now coming into sight, the pace of the yen's rise may slow in the near term on caution over MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 Kikuko Takeda, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at the Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi, says Y101.25 will soon be in sight, "as there's no major support up to that level." Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase Bank in Tokyo, predicts the dollar will end the year between Y98 and Y100, while Daisuke Uno, market analyst at Sumitomo Mitsui Banking, sees the dollar between Y100 and Y102.50 at the year-end.  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush, at the Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation forum summit in Santiago, reiterated the U.S. policy favoring a "strong dollar," but traders dismissed the remark as an attempt to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's downglide from turning into a free-fall - and as a sop to his Japanese ally, Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi. Bush also promised to halve the gigantic U.S. budget deficit, but analysts noted this too was nothing new.  \par \par \uc2 Rather, the market was more affected by Greenspan's comments that, "given the size of the U.S. current-account deficit, a diminished appetite for adding to dollar balances must occur at some point." Greenspan, addressing bankers Friday in Frankfurt, also belittled the idea of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  changing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trends: "Very large \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  don't create large increases in exchange rates of a protracted nature," he said. "I don't see a very clear impact on the exchange rate."  \par \par \uc2 While the U.S. was giving scant aid to the dollar, the APEC and G20 meetings also focused attention on U.S.-led global efforts to get China to let its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise. That could help U.S. exports a tad and also take some of the upward pressure off the euro.  \par \par \uc2 President Hu Jintao, meeting Bush in Santiago, promised to "pay more attention" to the issue of loosening the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's peg to the dollar, a U.S. official said, although he gave no new hints about when Beijing might unshackle the yuan. Indeed, at the G20, Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Zhou Xiachuan said Beijing must make more progress on the institutional reforms necessary to prepare for exchange rate liberalization.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. has been pushing China for more than a year to free up the yuan, also called the renminbi, arguing that China is keeping the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - and thus the nation's exports - artificially low, hurting U.S. manufacturers and robbing American jobs. China has long said its ultimate goal is a liberalized yuan, but officials have rebuffed any pressure to hurry.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing keeps the yuan in a tiny 0.3% range above or below CNY8.2770 to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Markets expect China to begin relaxing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime sometime in the coming months, such as by allowing a wider trading band for the yuan. That could prompt accelerated rises in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, where authorities already worry about competition from China.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar sank Monday in the offshore market for nondeliverable yuan forwards to its widest one-year discount since early March, 4,000 points, from Friday's 3,850. That means the market is now pricing in expectations that the U.S. unit will drop to CNY7.857 in the next 12 months, for a yuan rise of 5.3%. In late July the NDF market was forecasting just a 1.0% yuan rise over the ensuing 12 months.  \par \par \uc2 While a rise in the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could affect \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  across Asia, one increasingly being watched is the ringgit, which Malaysia pegged to the dollar in 1998 during the Asian financial crisis. Kuala Lumpur was widely thought to be awaiting a move from Beijing before touching the ringgit peg - and indeed Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi has recently said his government is in close contact with Beijing on the issue.  \par \par \uc2 But at the APEC gathering, Abdullah bolstered a new view in the markets that Malaysia may not even wait for a full-blown yuan revaluation to tinker with the ringgit peg.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't create that kind of linkage," the premier said, state-run Bernama news agency reported Monday. "If we decide to make a decision, we will take it on our own. It doesn't have to be linked to the yuan."  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com \par    (Takashi Nakamichi covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and economics and policy in Japan for Dow Jones Newswires in Tokyo.) \par    (Andrew Peaple in Berlin, Alex Keto in Santiago, Leika Kihara in Tokyo and Ron Harui in Singapore contributed to this item.) \par    -Edited by William Mallard \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-22-04 0156ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020041122e0bm0000k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
