\par\par \b \uc2 AWSJ(11/22) Revaluation Looms As Asia Awaits China Action  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,338 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 09:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 AWSJ(11/22) Revaluation Looms As Asia Awaits China Action  \par \par \uc2 (From THE ASIAN WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By Patrick Barta In Bangkok and andrew Browne and Mary Kissel In Hong Kong  \par \par \uc2 Asia appears to be in the early stages of a regionwide \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  revaluation, partly in anticipation that China will let its own \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float higher over the next year.  \par \par \uc2 From Tokyo to Seoul to Singapore, some investors are buying up large volumes of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a strategy that assumes Asian countries will tolerate somewhat stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the year ahead. The won is trading near its highest level against the dollar in seven years, while the yen is up nearly 7% since the beginning of October.  \par \par \uc2 Investors also are pouring capital into Asia's equity and bond markets -- a bet that appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will boost the value of their assets over time. Meanwhile, a number of Asia's largest companies are drafting strategies to deal with current and anticipated adjustments in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including cutting costs and changing the amount of business they do in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 'There is beginning to be a little smoke out of that volcano that we're going to see something' in terms of stronger Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the next year, says Frank Vargo, vice president for international economic affairs at the National Association of Manufacturers in Washington, which has long lobbied for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia.  \par \par \uc2 Further revaluation in Asia is still far from certain. With export growth slowing across Asia, many governments could decide that further revaluation is too risky as stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could make their exports less competitive. The governments of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and South Korea have said they might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too much. One day this month, the Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a bid to slow the strengthening of the won against the dollar, fearing that too much appreciation will hurt competitiveness of the country's exports.  \par \par \uc2 A sizable revaluation also could create problems for some Asian countries by cutting the value of their huge \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves, leading to hefty balance sheet losses. This is especially true for China, which has $515 billion of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings, equivalent to about 30% of the country's gross domestic product.  \par \par \uc2 Still, analysts say there are a number of reasons Asian governments may be becoming more tolerant of upward \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 For one thing, China has hinted that it is planning to relax its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's tight peg to the dollar as part of a strategy to give Beijing more leverage to cool its overheated economy and lessen tensions with the U.S. Beijing is likely to cap any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fluctuations within a certain 'small' range when it goes ahead with the change, which might involve linking the yuan to a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a person familiar with the government's thinking said. This person wouldn't define 'small,' but said 5% could count as small while 10% would be 'big.'  \par \par \uc2 If China does act, it could give other Asian governments more flexibility to follow suit. China's neighbors are wary of letting their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciate too much without a simultaneous increase in the yuan, as doing so would make their manufactured goods less competitive.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  also could help Asian governments take some of the sting out of high oil and commodity prices, by giving consumers and factory owners more purchasing power. Inflationary concerns are especially prevalent in South Korea, which is scrambling to avoid slipping into a stagflationary bog because of weak consumer demand and rising prices.  \par \par \uc2 Finally, many analysts believe there is a growing recognition in Asia that the global trade imbalance is unsustainable, and that some appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary to prevent more dramatic -- and uncontrolled -- moves later on. The recent slide of the dollar against the euro has highlighted those risks, analysts say.  \par \par \uc2 In a conference call with clients last week, Lehman Brothers global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Jim McCormick said he believes the yuan will be 5% higher by the end of 2005 while some other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  could rise as much as 15%.  \par \par \uc2 'Asia is willing to tolerate much stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  than most were thinking even a few weeks ago,' he said.  \par \par \uc2 In one way, Asia is experiencing a reversal of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis that swept the region in 1997. Then, Beijing helped to prevent a greater rout of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by pledging not to devalue, turning the yuan into an anchor of regional stability. Now, the region is looking to China to pull up the anchor so that its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  can rise in value, a sign the region has weathered the storm and is ready to sail on.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, in some ways a further decoupling of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the dollar could help aid the region's march to a healthier economy. To ensure their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  move somewhat in tandem with the dollar, Asian central banks have been buying dollars and piling up \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. If they allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise, they will buy fewer dollars, in effect keeping more Asian savings at home, a move that could deepen capital markets in the region.  \par \par \uc2 A modest revaluation of Asia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't solve the gaping U.S current-account deficit. That deficit -- the shortfall on trade and investment income between the U.S. and the rest of the world -- has widened because the U.S. economy imports far more goods than it exports, forcing the U.S. to borrow heavily from China, Japan and elsewhere to finance its purchases. As the U.S. falls deeper into debt, economists warn, some investors might decide to pull their money from U.S. markets. That could trigger a sharp fall in U.S. asset prices and a sudden surge in interest rates, which in turn might cause a global recession.  \par \par \uc2 But even slightly revalued \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would represent an important symbolic shift for Asian countries, many of which have kept their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at artificially low levels since at least the late 1990s. Analysts believe some Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are between 10% and 20% below the levels at which they would trade floated freely.  \par \par \uc2 Many Asian companies are proceeding as if further revaluations are inevitable. As it puts together its business plan for next year, for example, Korea's LG Electronics Co. says it believes the yuan will appreciate by between 5% and 10% next year, but it is preparing for a rise of as much as 15% just to be safe.  \par \par \uc2 Among other things, the company is trying to reduce its exposure to dollar-denominated trade. This year, about 80% of LG's export trade has been conducted in dollars, with about 10% in euros and the rest in other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Next year, the company is trying to cut its reliance on dollars to 70% and increase its euro-denominated trade to 20%. LG also has swapped some debt denominated in won for dollar-denominated debt, which it believes will be less costly as the dollar depreciates.  \par \par \uc2 Investors, meanwhile, are moving money into Asian stocks and bonds.  \par \par \uc2 One of the most popular trades is to buy Malaysian government bonds denominated in ringgit in the expectation that the country will unpeg its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the dollar over the next year, allowing it to appreciate. Malaysian Prime Minister Abdullah Ahmad Badawi said this month that there is no plan to unpeg the ringgit from the dollar, but many analysts believe such a move is inevitable.  \par \par \uc2 Many investors have been selling shares of some of the region's biggest exporters, however, a reminder that any meaningful revaluation would likely cause significant pain for many Asian companies in the short run. On Friday, Samsung Electronics fell 2% in Seoul, partly on worries that the won might become too strong.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 Gordon Fairclough and Seah Park in Seoul and Sebastian Moffett in Tokyo contributed to this article.  \par \par \uc2 (See related article: 'U.S. President's Move At Summit Follows Greenspan's Warning' -- AWSJ Nov. 22, 2004)  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041122e0bm0050p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
