\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  OPTION VIEWS: Sitting With 3-Month USD/JPY Volatility  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,280 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 November 2004\par 08:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Russell Floyd \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The dollar looks headed for more weakness after a series of multiyear lows, stoking the yen-bullishness of the options market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 But with the Japanese authorities strongly threatening to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support to dollar and crush any "erratic" movements in the near term, it makes sense to go long volatility a little further out the curve.  \par \par \uc2 Owning three-month dollar-yen volatilities looks particularly appealing. At the same time, knock-outs under the market for an extended euro-dollar rise, and binary-type structures for a strengthening South Korean won may be appropriate.  \par \par \uc2 Comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan and increased Chinese yuan-revaluation talk helped knock the dollar to a four-and-a-half year low of Y102.72 on Friday and a seven-year low Monday of 1055.8 won, while the euro last week rallied to a record $1.3075. Around 0730 GMT the dollar was at Y103.32 and KRW1,065.2, while the euro was at $1.3037.  \par \par \uc2 In yen options, the spread of 25-delta USD puts/JPY calls over USD calls/JPY puts rose - to 1.30% on Friday in the one-month and 1.90% in the one-year, gains of 0.30% and 0.20%, respectively, for the week.  \par \par \uc2 Although the one-month risk reversal's bias for yen calls is 1.00 standard deviation above its 12-month mean of 0.79%, it's still not abnormally expensive - it can spike to 2% at times of real stress. But the spreads are wide enough that it becomes somewhat difficult to buy low-delta yen calls, particularly if the view is for the Japanese authorities to attempt to contain USD/JPY downside.  \par \par \uc2 And indeed Japan gave its strongest warning yet Sunday that it might step in.  \par \par \uc2 "Now is the proper time for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start thinking about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Hiroshi Watanabe told Dow Jones Newswires on the sidelines of a Berlin meeting of Group of 20 finance officials. "The movement in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the last seven days has been rapid and erratic, meaning this is the proper time to think of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That is our basic principle."  \par \par \uc2 Watanabe didn't state a target USD/JPY level, but market participants think the authorities may buy the dollar just above Y101.  \par \par \uc2 While his comments helped stabilize the dollar Monday, they don't turn off the heat entirely; further dollar weakness is all but assured.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan encouraged global dollar bears when he said the huge U.S. current-account deficit means "diminished appetite for adding to dollar balances must occur at some point," and when he belittled the idea that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can change \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trends.  \par \par \uc2 To play for further tension in USD/JPY, the three-month volatility looks attractive, even though risk reversals maintain a significant bias for yen calls.  \par \par \uc2 The volatility implied by three-month options prices was 9.53% on Friday, up 0.63 percentage point for the week but still not extreme in terms of what we could expect from the spot market under normal circumstances. Over the last year, USD/JPY has displayed a mean three-month volatility of 9.44%. So on average we would expect this position to roughly break even.  \par \par \uc2 The idea is to be long volatility but to do so at an expiry that can stand a little decay while we wait to see what happens. With Japanese and U.S. holidays this week, there might be a concern that spot sits for a bit; the three-month straddle can handle this. Since the gamma is not as high as on a one-month straddle, it will decay at a slower rate, which makes it a sensible strategy for sitting in front of potential action.  \par \par \uc2 The position also looks attractive on the back of action in the three-month historical volatility. After falling more than 6 vols in a near straight line over the last five months, the three-month series has bounced from 5.5% on Nov. 11 to 6.32% on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 It's the first real sign of a reversal of this downtrend since it began in July and should be encouraging to USD/JPY volatility bulls.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par              Korean Won Volatilities Spike \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the dollar weakened across Asia last week, its sharpest moves were against the Korean won, which traded to a string of seven-year highs after the authorities failed to deliver a strong stance on the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minister of Finance and Economy Lee Hun-Jai spoke at length Thursday on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  issues but his admission the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate should be market-determined gave the green light to traders to sell dollars. The dollar bounced back a tad Monday after the government asked the Bank of Korea to print more won to sell for dollars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 One-month at-the-money-forward USD/KRW volatility was quoted at 9.70% Friday after hitting 9.75% the day before, up from the previous Friday's 5.95% and 3.85% a month ago. The spot action generated significant demand for won calls, pushing the risk reversal to a hefty KRW-call bias of 2.20% in the one-month Friday, up from just 0.35% a month ago.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This strength in the won and the buildup in won option prices support the notion of playing for continued dollar falls against Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But bids by the Seoul authorities could slow things down somewhat.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We favor trades that sell USD/KRW, but with option prices at these heights, it might be better to stick with spot trades and tight stops for now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alternatively, binary-type structures, like one-month in-the-money USD put/KRW call spreads, which have short USD/KRW delta and low gamma/vega risk, seem appropriate.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Knock-Out EUR Calls/USD Puts \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 We have been advocates of reverse knock-out EUR calls/USD puts in front of grinding EUR/USD spot gains, but traders looking to capture an extension of dollar weakness against the single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  might want to consider placing knock-outs in the other direction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the expectation is for euro gains to slow - as European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  concern over the euro's rise provides a ceiling, and the spot pair grinds to a level and stops - the premium of EUR calls can be reduced by setting strikes in the path of the EUR/USD rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But in the wake of the G20 meeting - at which only Japan appeared set to fight the dollar's fall - traders may feel more confident that EUR/USD can run beyond levels previously imagined. They might prefer to place the knock-outs under the market. Conventional knock-outs are best suited to trending \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs, as the risk of knock-out is a function of the risk of a correction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For reference, if you think EUR/USD will extend higher and that any pullback will be limited by the 1.2900 level, you can own a one-month 1.3036 EUR call/USD put with a 1.2900 knock-out for 96 USD points, compared to 137 for the vanilla.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     -By Russell Floyd, russell.floyd@vollab.com \par  \par    (Russell Floyd is an independent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options strategist who may have positions in some of the products discussed above. Opinions expressed are those of the author, not of Dow Jones Newswires.) \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Market Data provided by GFI Group, NY ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gfigroup.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.gfigroup.com}}}\uc2 \f2 ) \par    Option Pricing by Fenics \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  provided by GFI Group, NY ( \f28 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.gfigroup.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.gfigroup.com}}}\uc2 \f2 )   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Lim Mui Khi and William Mallard [ 11-22-04 0229ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1180  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041122e0bm0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
