\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 2-Japan keeps up verbal warnings on rapid  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  moves.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki   \par \uc2 788 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2004\par 23:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 BERLIN, Nov 20 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept up verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Saturday to try to curb the export-damaging rise in the yen, pledging decisive action on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary.   \par \par \uc2 Speaking on the sidelines of a Group of 20 meeting of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors and finance ministers in Berlin, Japan's top financial diplomat Hiroshi Watanabe said recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have been rapid and he was watching carefully.   \par \par \uc2 "As for Japan, we think recent current movements are very rapid and we are watching carefully. We will take decisive action if necessary," said Watanabe, who is Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs at the Finance Ministry.   \par \par \uc2 The yen hit a 4-1/2 year high of 102.67 per dollar on Friday ahead of the G20 meeting as the greenback tumbled after comments from U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan.   \par \par \uc2 Echoing Watanabe, Ryotaro Tanose, senior Japanese vice finance minister, said rapid moves were undesirable.   \par \par \uc2 "Excessive, disorderly \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves are not good for the world's economy. We are monitoring carefully. Japan will take decisive action if necessary," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Japan, which spent around $330 billion trying to fight the dollar's slide between January 2003 and March 2004, has repeatedly warned it would act if needed but so far there have been no signs of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Analysts say the remarks did not increase the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the economy has grown more resilient to a stronger dollar than last year.   \par \par \uc2 "The tone of those statements sounds similar to those of other Japanese officials last week. There's nothing new. I don't think they raise the probability of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.   \par \par \uc2 "Unless we test 100 next week or a corrective rally occurs naturally I don't think they will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 . I don't see them tryng to defend a line in the sand."   \par \par \uc2 He said that last year \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had entrenched deflation and was happy to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports and increase money supply. "Now they have... price stability and they'll be happy to have a stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, which floods the money market to keep interest rates near zero, expects Japan's five-year bout with deflation is likely to end next year. The bank has pledged to keep its ultra-easy monetary policy until consumer prices steadied above zero.   \par \par \uc2 OIL RISKS LIMITED   \par \par \uc2 Japanese monetary officials are worried a rapidly rising yen could derail a recovery in the export-oriented economy.   \par \par \uc2 Oil prices, which hit a record high over $50 a barrel earlier this year, were also seen as a risk to the world's second-largest economy as Japan imports all of its oil.   \par \par \uc2 Watanabe said oil prices were still high but the direct impact from costly oil was limited.   \par \par \uc2 "(Oil prices are) still at around elevated levels of $45-46 (per barrel). In case of Japan... even though we have some negative impact from the very high petroleum prices we have less dependency on the petroleum if you compared the situation with the 1970s," he said.   \par \par \uc2 "We also import cheaper oil from the Gulf. So the direct impact to our economy is limited. But we are so much interacted with Asian economies and in some Asian countries like China (oil) would have a negative impact, in which case we will have an indirect impact."   \par \par \uc2 However, recent data suggested a slowdown in global demand is choking Japan's export-reliant recovery.   \par \par \uc2 Last week, both the government and the BOJ downgraded their views on the economy as slowing exports and output cast a shadow over an ongoing recovery.   \par \par \uc2 The move follows data this month that showed the world's second-largest economy grew just 0.1 percent in July-September.   \par \par \uc2 That translated into annualised growth of 0.3 percent, a sharp slowdown from over six percent late last year and early this year and well below U.S. third-quarter growth of 3.7 percent.   \par \par \uc2 When asked about the Japan's economy, Tanose said: "It's moving steadily this fiscal year. The question is whether we can maintain this next year. I have a good expectation for next fiscal year."   \par \par \uc2 The dollar, under pressure in recent weeks, took a hit on Friday after Federal Reserve chairman Alan Greenspan said it seemed persuasive that, given the size of the U.S. current account deficit, a diminished appetite for adding to dollar balances must occur at some point.   \par \par \uc2 Greenspan also said that very large \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not create very large increases in exchange rates of a protracted nature although there was some impact.   \par \par \uc2 - Additional Reporting by Charlotte Cooper.    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041120e0bk00ac9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
