\par\par \b \uc2 Germany's Schroeder: Euro-Dollar Relationship Worrying  \b0\par\par\uc2 665 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2004\par 12:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 BERLIN (Dow Jones)--German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder Saturday called the exchange rate between the dollar and the euro "worrying."  \par \par \uc2 Speaking to reporters on the sidelines of the meeting of officials from the Group of 20 industrialized and emerging-market countries, Schroeder said, "The euro-dollar relation is worrying. The reason is clearly the U.S. twin deficit, so the current account deficit and the budget deficit."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell sharply against the euro and the yen again Friday after U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan warned of the risks of running large deficits. [ 20-11-04 1251GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Regarding global imbalances such as the U.S. deficits, Schroeder said "partnership stands for taking measures."  \par \par \uc2 "One can't repeatedly call on the Europeans to implement structural reforms, which we are doing, and not point out their own economic necessities," Schroeder said.  \par \par \uc2 He also indicated that central banks should consider taking action regarding current \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate movements.  \par \par \uc2 "The European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other central banks will certainly think about any possibilities to take action," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at an all-time low against the euro on Friday, and hit a four-and-a-half year low against the yen after Greenspan downplayed the effectiveness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 Still, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Vice Finance Minister Hiroshi Watanabe told reporters in Berlin that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it believes it's necessary.  \par \par \uc2 Schroeder called for closer coordination with Asia, particularly with China and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 He also said that a final agreement over Iraq's debt to the Paris Club of sovereign creditors has not yet been reached.  \par \par \uc2 "There is no final outcome so far, there are discussions, particularly with France," he said. "We have to point out that conditionality has to be applied in the last stage of what has been agreed or what will be agreed."  \par \par \uc2 He also indicated that the Iraqi debt write off might be reassessed again in future.  \par \par \uc2 "If the situation in Iraq improves fundamentally - Iraq is a rich country with respect to its oil reserves - we should be able to talk about it again."  \par \par \uc2 Earlier Saturday, German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said that he and U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow have reached an agreement under which Iraq's creditors would write off up to 80% of Iraq's debt.  \par \par \uc2 "We agreed that there should be a write-off of debts in several stages amounting to 80% in total," Eichel said. "Within this framework, the necessary decisions can now be taken in the Paris Club," Eichel said.  \par \par \uc2 The 80% will be forgiven in three steps, Eichel said.  \par \par \uc2 In a first step, 30% would be waived immediately. In a second step, a further 30% write-off would be linked to a program between the International Monetary Fund and Iraq to replace the current post-conflict assistance program after it expires.  \par \par \uc2 In a third step, the remaining 20% will depend on Iraq's successful implementation of the IMF program, said Eichel, who met privately with Snow early Saturday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. had proposed the Paris Club write off 95% of the roughly $42 billion Iraq owes it, a sum that includes about $20 billion of past-due interest accumulated during the 1990s when Iraq was under international sanctions.  \par \par \uc2 The French government, backed by the Russians, has proposed the Paris Club write off just half of the debt, postpone debt service for three years, and revisit the issue when Iraq's economy is in better condition. Officials from France, Russia and Germany have argued that Iraq, with its large oil resources, shouldn't receive debt relief terms more favorable than the world's poorest countries.  \par \par \uc2 The International Monetary Fund estimates that Iraq's total foreign debt amounts to $120 billion, six times the size of its economy.  \par \par \uc2 -By Christian Vits, Dow Jones Newswires; djnews.berlin@dowjones.com; +44 49 30 288 8410  \par \par \uc2 (Andrea Thomas in Berlin contributed to this report) [ 20-11-04 1321GMT ]  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041120e0bk00046\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
