\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Big Day For Central Bankers  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2004\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 US SUMMARY: Stocks Edge Out A Gain  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par DJIA          10572.55  gain 22.98      up 0.2% \par NASDAQ        2104.28   gain 4.60       up 0.2% \par S&P 500       1186.55   gain 1.61       up 0.1% \par Dow Future    10577.00  loss 10.00      dn 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1579.50   loss 3.00       dn 0.2% \par S&P Future    1183.75   loss 1.75       dn 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.12% down 0.03 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Stocks rose modestly in uninspired trading Thursday as investors looked past a mix of economic data and falling profit forecasts from Google Inc. and Applied Materials Inc. Technical factors boosted Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: While Applied Materials' forecasts were understandable given high inventories in semiconductors, Google's warning that its current strong profits were unsustainable in future quarters surprised some investors.  \par \par \uc2 Wall Street was cheered, however, by the Labor Department's report on first-time unemployment claims, which fell by 3,000 to 334,000 last week, the lowest level since the end of October. And while trading remained sluggish and the market's gains were minimal, investors and analysts remained bullish.  \par \par \uc2 "The economic data (Thursday) was a little weaker than expected, but investors remain upbeat about the market right now," said Michael Sheldon, chief market strategist at Spencer Clarke LLC. "The market is somewhat overbought on a short-term basis, but any pullback will be shallow and short lived."  \par \par \uc2 Despite recent troubles in the tech sector, Intel Corp. was bullish Thursday about its own potential. Chief executive Craig Barrett, at a conference in New Delhi, said he expected much improved profit growth in the first half of 2005. Walt Disney Co.'s fiscal fourth-quarter net income surged 24%, aided by strong gains at Disney's TV networks and a favorable tax settlement.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Long-dated government bond prices staged a late rally Thursday on a mixture of forces, in a day in which the market got of lot of economic data but showed only a modest reaction to it.  \par \par \uc2 Josh Stiles, a strategist with IDEAGlobal in New York, noted that the front end of the market is likely locked down to reflect shifting Federal Reserve expectations that are moving to price in a course of more aggressive rate hikes, along with movements to price upcoming two year note supply. As for the curve, there are technical factors at play there, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Taken together the factors focused on by markets "tend to flatten out the curve a little bit," Stiles said, adding that the narrowing spreads were "notable" for the extent of their move.  \par \par \uc2 Andy Brenner, head of fixed income at Investec in New York, told clients that "economic numbers do not matter at this point in time. It is a technical market."  \par \par \uc2 In the day's data, the Federal Reserve Bank of Philadelphia said that its business conditions index, a gauge of the health of the region's manufacturing sector, moved down to a reading of 20.7 for November, versus the 28.5 it stood at in October. November's reading was below the 23.5 predicted by economists. The bank said that despite the slowing, the data "remain at relatively high levels."  \par \par \uc2 More ominously, the Conference Board said that the economy's outlook in October softened, and that its composite index of leading indicators fell 0.3% in October to 115.1, versus the revised 0.3% decline reported for September. October's reading fell short of the 0.1% decline predicted by economists.  \par \par \uc2 "A fifth straight decline in the leading indicators is a clear signal that the economy is losing steam, and may start off 2005 with a relatively weak pace of economic activity," said Conference Board economist Ken Goldstein. In the report, the group warned that "the recent declines in the leading index have not been large enough nor have they persisted for long enough to signal an end to the current expansion."  \par \par \uc2 ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Nudge Higher  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par USD-Yen             104.18       loss  0.02    dn 0.02% \par AUD-USD             0.7760       loss  0.0020  dn 0.3% \par Nikkei 225          11106.48     gain  24.06   up 0.2% \par Hang Seng           13760.66     loss  39.16   dn 0.3% \par JGB Yield           1.4350% up 0.0050 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar held steady against the Japanese yen Friday morning as investors cautiously watched for signs of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after the greenback fell to nearly an eight-month low the day before.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, Japan's Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said current market conditions still require close monitoring.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If there is a need to, we will resolutely do what we have to do," Tanigaki said, referring to the possibility \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance authorities would conduct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if there was too much speculative activity on the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo shares rose Friday morning on the back of the dollar's stability against the yen. A stronger yen is a concern because it hurts earnings of Japanese exporters.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The price of crude stayed put in Asia on Friday as traders took stock of falling U.S. distillate supplies and a stepped-up offensive in the criminal probe of Russian oil giant OAO Yukos that threatens to shut down the company.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Light, sweet crude for December on the New York Mercantile Exchange fell 2 cents from its overnight closing to $46.20 per barrel midmorning in Asia Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Markets Set For Mixed Start  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Euro-USD    1.2962      gain  0.0004  up 0.03% \par Stlg-USD    1.8518      gain  0.0020  up 0.1% \par USD-Franc   1.1703      loss  0.0010  dn 0.08% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European shares are set for a mixed opening with government debt and the euro little changed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: Prices are likely to open mixed as investors sort out whether stocks have more upside potential or are due for a correction lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. spreadbettor IG Index is forecasting a two-point fall on the FTSE to 4803 but sees the DAX opening up 2 at 4180 with the CAC unchanged at 3830.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks ended flat to lower Thursday, despite reassuring corporate newsflow, as investors began to question whether equities can continue to march higher into the year end.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Dollars and oil remain the market's principal concerns," said Anais Faraj, strategist at Nomura. Others in the market are optimistic of more upside to come.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Akber Khan, of Deutsche Bank European equity Focus team noted that there is a lot of liquidity in the market that needs to be invested.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Portfolios are at present structured against growth. However, economists are now more optimistic over global growth prospects given the retreating oil price, hence fund managers are having to play catch up," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Investors will likely give bonds a rest after recent volatility, and ahead of a host of central bankers speaking later Friday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European government bonds posted strong gains early Thursday, but eventually succumbed to a bout of profit-taking, ending sharply lower.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "People are very happy to keep being short," said ABN Amro's Graham McDevitt at the bank's 2005 global outlook conference call. "The market is willing to get short on any (bond) negative data."  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro opens little changed with central bankers potentially liable to say something on the low dollar, which could bounce higher on any threat to curb its weakness.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar strengthened from a fresh all-time low against the euro Thursday as investors took profits after several days of heavy losses for the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 John McCarthy, director of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at ING Barings Capital Markets, said profit taking was the big motive.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "(The dollar's correction) been spurred by slightly better economic data, but really, it's just a reason to take the money off table," he said. "A lot of people had some very good runs. They've been long euros ... and essentially what they're doing is unwinding those positions."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 McCarthy said he'd be surprised if the euro drops much below the $1.2930/40 level, saying there's "pretty decent support," which may see investors pile back into dollar selling at that level.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CALENDAR: Friday, Nov 19: Central Bankers Speak  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par GMT                                                         Expected  Previous \par 0745  FRA  3Q      Prelim GDP                               +0.4% QQ  +0.7% QQ \par                                                             +2.4% YY  +3.0% YY \par 0750  FRA  Oct     CPI                                      +0.4% MM  +0.1% MM \par                                                             +2.3% YY  +2.1% YY \par 0830  ITA  Sep     Industrial orders                        +1.8% YY  +9.7% YY \par 0830  ITA  Sep     I