\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Drops Below Y103 As Selloff Continues  \b0\par\par\uc2 615 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2004\par 15:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 New York (Dow Jones)--The dollar fell below Y103 in the New York morning session Friday, reaching a four-and-a-half year low against the yen as a broad dollar selloff continued.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar dropped against most widely traded counterparts, as traders used remarks by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan as an excuse to sell the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At 10.00 a.m. EST (1500 GMT), the dollar was at Y102.95 from Y104.19 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.3046 from $1.2959 late Thursday. The dollar was fetching CHF1.1598 from CHF1.1626 late Thursday, while the pound was at $1.8573 against $1.8506.  \par \par \uc2 At the opening of New York trading, the dollar crashed through its previous low for the year of Y103.44 and continued falling to Y102.92 from Y104.19 late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 There has been speculation that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at these levels to stem the yen's rise. The BOJ has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Japanese government to hinder the yen's appreciation in order to keep Japanese export goods competitive in world markets, but hasn't been active in markets since March of this year.  \par \par \uc2 In his remarks at a European Bankers conference in Frankfurt, Greenspan played down concerns about the current account deficit. He also said \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  had no clear impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet is also due to speak.  \par \par \uc2 The euro initially rose against the dollar moving near the high it reached earlier this week. However, while the yen continued to climb, the euro edged back somewhat and fell against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Andy Busch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Harris Nesbitt in Chicago, said that the market seized on Greenspan's comments to sell dollars because the Fed chairman's remarks can be interpreted as indicating U.S. authorities are too sanguine about the impact of the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 However, Busch noted that if Greenspan had said the opposite - that the dollar was bound to sink because of the deficit - then investors would have sold the dollar even more heavily.  \par \par \uc2 "Everything that comes out is being viewed through the same prism: sell dollars," said Busch.  \par \par \uc2 " It underscores how strong the trend is for (selling) the dollar. We're in this timeframe now where everything that is negative is viewed as negative and sold, but everything that's positive, the market gets a slight rally," he said, noting the dollar then falls back again. Fears about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Japanese gained further fuel overnight, with Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki telling reporters that "current conditions need to be watched with great caution ... If there is a need to, we will resolutely do what we have to do."  \par \par \uc2 Officials also said Japan will likely revise downward its third quarter gross domestic product to a contraction of 0.1%, from growth of 0.3%. That could boost the case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations is meeting in Berlin and a summit of leaders of the Asian-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum starts Friday in Chile.  \par \par \uc2 It is thought \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters will be discussed at these meetings, though European officials have already played down expectations of any joint position on the recent bout of dollar weakness at the G20 meeting. President George W. Bush is expected to discuss China's exchange rate regime with Chinese President Hu Jintao at the APEC summit.  \par \par \uc2 -By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com [ 11-19-04 1000ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041119e0bj0006z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
