\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Hits Year Low Against Yen As Greenspan Speaks  \b0\par\par\uc2 764 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2004\par 14:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 New York (Dow Jones)--The dollar reached a low for 2004 versus the yen in early New York trading Friday, as traders used remarks by Federal Reserve Chairman Alan Greenspan as an excuse to sell the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to Y103.18, crashing through the previous low for the year of Y103.43 reached in late March. The yen was at Y104.19 late Thursday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's fall also left the euro near a fresh all-time high amid broad losses for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro is at $1.3052, though it reached as high as $1.3067, from $1.2959 late Thursday. That's just off the $1.3074 high it reached in Asian trading early Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 At 9.00 a.m. EST (1400 GMT), the dollar was fetching CHF1.1596, from CHF1.1626 late Thursday, while the pound was at $1.8598 against $1.8506.  \par \par \uc2 After weakening overnight, the dollar dipped sharply once Greenspan's speech to a conference of European bankers in Frankfurt was released. According to traders, there's nothing immediately obvious in the text of Greenspan's speech to serve as a trigger for a selloff.  \par \par \uc2 His comments actually downplayed concerns about the U.S. current account deficit, saying there's no evidence that the gap is hard to finance. Greenspan also said that reducing budget deficit will lower risk of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. Greenspan doesn't believe such a crisis is pre-ordained.  \par \par \uc2 Greenspan is appearing along with European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet.  \par \par \uc2 There has been some speculation among traders that a new year high for the yen may trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the Japanese government. The BOJ last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in March.  \par \par \uc2 Corrected November 19, 2004 09:24 ET (1424GMT) [ 11-19-04 0904ET ]  \par \par \uc2 After weakening overnight, the dollar dipped sharply once Greenspan's speech to a conference of European bankers in Frankfurt was released.  \par \par \uc2 (In an item timed at 9:04 a.m. EST (1404 GMT), the city where Greenspan is speaking was misidentified.) [ 11-19-04 0916ET ]  \par \par \uc2 Andy Busch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Harris Nesbitt in Chicago, said that the market seized on Greenspan's comments to sell dollars because the Fed chairman's remarks can be interpreted as indicating U.S. authorities are too sanguine about the impact of the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 However, Busch noted that if Greenspan had said the opposite - that the dollar was bound to sink because of the deficit - then investors would have sold the dollar even more heavily.  \par \par \uc2 "Everything that comes out is being viewed through the same prism: sell dollars," said Busch.  \par \par \uc2 " It underscores how strong the trend is for (selling) the dollar. We're in this timeframe now where everything that is negative is viewed as negative and sold, but everything that's positive, the market gets a slight rally," he said, noting the dollar then falls back again. Fears about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Japanese gained further fuel overnight, with Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki telling reporters that "current conditions need to be watched with great caution ... If there is a need to, we will resolutely do what we have to do."  \par \par \uc2 Officials also said Japan will likely revise downward its third quarter Gross Domestic Product to a contraction of 0.1%, from growth of 0.3%. That could boost the case for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The other main events Friday are also speakers. Trichet's speech on the panel with Greenspan will be closely watched, while several regional Fed presidents are also speaking.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Group of 20 industrial and developing nations is meeting in Berlin and a summit of leaders of the Asian-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum starts Friday in Chile.  \par \par \uc2 It is thought \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters will be discussed at these meetings, though European officials have already played down expectations of any joint position on the recent bout of dollar weakness at the G20 meeting. President George W. Bush is expected to discuss China's exchange rate regime with Chinese President Hu Jintao at the APEC summit.  \par \par \uc2 At 9.40 a.m. EST (1400 GMT), the dollar was at Y102.93 from Y104.19 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.3046 from $1.2959 late Thursday. The dollar was fetching CHF1.1598 from CHF1.1626 late Thursday, while the pound was at $1.8573 against $1.8506.  \par \par \uc2 By Laurence Norman, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com [ 11-19-04 0944ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041119e0bj0006o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
