\par\par \b \uc2 OFF THE RUN: The Economy? Treasury Bid Could Care Less  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,016 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2004\par 20:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Agnes T. Crane \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Don't even try explaining it, at least not by looking at the U.S. economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 That's what one Treasury trader said this week when the market rallied in the face of a string of strong economic data, higher stocks, lower oil prices and a host of other so-called fundamental factors that under "normal" circumstances would have hammered government securities.  \par \par \uc2 In typical fashion, bond market analysts prepared lists of reasons for the counterintuitive rally to give clients who, by and large, have been frustrated by the market's unwillingness to play by the most basic rule for the better part of 2004: stronger economic growth and Federal Reserve rate hikes mean higher Treasury yields and lower prices.  \par \par \uc2 Some talked up the ever-present covering of short, or bearish positions, that erupts when strong economic data fails to cause the expected wave of selling. Others pointed to the weak dollar and the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  as fueling a bid for government securities.  \par \par \uc2 The one factor, however, that seemed to gain the most traction among traders was a mysterious asset allocation trade that allegedly pulled money out of European bonds to pump them into Treasurys.  \par \par \uc2 The surge in trading volumes in bond markets on both sides of the Atlantic on Tuesday and Wednesday triggered the speculation that a hedge fund or a large institutional bond fund was behind the trade, although it was difficult to find someone who actually saw the flows.  \par \par \uc2 This explanation sounded a lot like the one given earlier this month when the 337,000 gain in October nonfarm payrolls failed to push the benchmark 10-year note through the top end of a broad 4.0% to 4.30% trading range. At that time, traders said a wave of buying from foreign central banks, Japan's and China's were the two prime suspects, capped the rise in yields over the following few sessions.  \par \par \uc2 By Thursday, long-dated yields were heading even lower.  \par \par \uc2 It's difficult to pin down exactly who is executing the trades that market participants estimate to be in the billions, but this persistent bid has in effect emasculated the much talked about bond vigilantes who have slinked away to the sidelines to wait for the end of fiscal year end, which for many is at the end of November.  \par \par \uc2 "Those who have made enough money are done and those who lost a lot of money are frozen," said Michael Cheah, portfolio manager at AIG SunAmerica Asset Management.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      Haven't Folded Yet \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 According to leading surveys, many portfolio managers had positioned their funds for higher interest rates throughout this year. Such a wager seemed like a no-brainer in January and again in April and May, but the benchmark 10-year yield has steadily dropped from its 4.90% high watermark in June to trade around 4.13% on Thursday, about 10 basis points lower than where it started the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Though investors have pared back their more bearish bets, they haven't folded yet, said Sadakichi Robbins, head of global fixed income at Bank Julius Baer in New York. "Part of the problem is the short base has yet to relent,"  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With so many anticipating and positioned for rising rates, there's less pent up pressure to sell Treasurys when data point to strong economic growth or rising inflation, traditionally the two big bear factors for bonds. And when the sellers don't show up, opportunistic buyers and foreign central banks do, with the latter doing so fairly consistently, according to traders.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "When central banks come in, they shouldn't define where interest rates go in the future," said Robbins. Foreign official institution purchases have less to do with price and more to do with the need to park an overflow of dollars in a safe haven instrument. But, with so many traditional investors who do care about value out of the market, these banks are essentially "running the show," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The steep decline in the dollar against its major rivals has also kept foreign central banks front and center in bond traders minds. If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  slides further, particularly against the yen, bond and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants speculate Japan's government will be forced back into \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets after an 8-month hiatus to stop a strong yen from derailing the nation's fragile export-led economy. Since Japan tends to funnel these dollar purchases into the Treasury market, such action would most likely keep the foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bid alive and well.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, a weakening U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  means China must buy more dollars to maintain the yuan's defacto peg. Many of these dollars are also channeled into the U.S. government debt market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another technical driver supporting Treasurys is the approach of the end of the year. Portfolio managers are apt to add government securities to their holdings before they close their books for the year to bring their weightings back to even after leaning short for most of 2004.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You still have a lot of people underweighted in bonds," said Cheah. And with Thanksgiving - the kickoff to the U.S. holiday season - only one week away, and Fed policy for the rest of the year already priced in, the much anticipated backup in yields isn't likely to happen anytime soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're in a trading range," said Andrew Brenner, head of institutional fixed income at Investec USA in New York, who doesn't expect the 10-year yield to breach 4.125% in the near term or 4.0% for that matter by the end of the month. But, on the other side, 4.28% to 4.30% has proven a solid ceiling.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes T. Crane covers U.S. and global credit markets for Dow Jones Newswires.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Agnes T. Crane, Dow Jones Newswires, 201-938-2122; \par agnes.crane@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Oliver Biggadike in Frankfurt contributed to this article) [ 18-11-04 1902GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041118e0bi00105\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
