\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(11/18) Dollar Gets Chill From Snow  \b0\par\par\uc2 767 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2004\par 07:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WSJ(11/18) Dollar Gets Chill From Snow  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By Michael R. Sesit  \par \par \uc2 Paris -- THE DOLLAR was Snowed under yesterday, tumbling to its lowest level against the euro in the European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's five-year life after comments by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow. But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets attentions increasingly are turning toward the Chinese yuan in anticipation of two high-profile meetings this weekend.  \par \par \uc2 The finance ministers and central bankers of the so-called G-20 industrial and big developing nations meet in Berlin, while the heads of state of Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation members meet in Santiago, Chile.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts are watching for any sign that the U.S., with European backing, will increase pressure on China and other Asian countries to subject their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to market forces. The U.S. hopes such a move would push those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  up against the dollar, while the Europeans hope it would relieve some of the upward pressure on their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 According to a senior administration official, President Bush will press Chinese President Hu Jintao at the APEC meeting to move forward with plans to allow China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float against the dollar. Mr. Bush made similar comments at last year's meeting. Since then, China has taken some small steps to prepare its economy for a possible float of the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 Wrangling over the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  comes as the dollar's woes continue. In the past 10 weeks, the dollar has weakened 5.6% against the euro, 4.6% against the yen and 2.8% against Britain's pound.  \par \par \uc2 Still, while the decline has been significant and a further weakening in the dollar is expected, many strategists say the dollar's level still isn't reaching catastrophic proportions. So far this year, the dollar has weakened less than 3% against the yen and less than 4% against the pound. Meanwhile, the euro has climbed less than 3.5% against the dollar in 2004 -- a much smaller movement than the euro posted against the dollar in any of the past three years.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, based on purchasing power parity, or the cost of a set basket of goods in different \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , 'the dollar has overshot to the downside' and 'will ultimately revert back toward its fair value,' said Mark Tinker, global head of strategy at stockbrokers Execution Ltd. in London. He figures that fair value is about $1.60 for the pound and $1.09 for the euro, implying a dollar that should be 16% to 19% stronger.  \par \par \uc2 In late New York trading yesterday, the euro strengthened to $1.3035 from $1.2957 late Tuesday. The dollar weakened to 103.98 yen from 105.39 yen, and to 1.1628 Swiss francs from 1.1767 francs. The pound strengthened to $1.8584 from $1.8528. Earlier in the day, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slid to a 12-year low against the Canadian dollar, a near nine-year nadir against the Swiss franc and its lowest point in seven months against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 In a speech at the Royal Institute of International Affairs in London, Mr. Snow reiterated that the administration has a 'strong dollar' policy, adding, 'Our dollar policy remains unchanged because a strong dollar is in both the national and international interest.'  \par \par \uc2 He also suggested the dollar's level should be valued by the market, rather than by governments or international accords, saying, 'the only way to get prosperity is to follow the marketplace.' Analysts noted that, unlike earlier U.S. administrations, the Bush administration has yet to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  even once in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to try to influence the dollar's value.  \par \par \uc2 Catalysts that could stabilize the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  include rising U.S. interest rates, which increase the appeal of U.S. assets to international investors, and resumed \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- or dollar buying -- by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and possibly even the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 So why are traders persisting in driving down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the world's fastest-growing major economy, while pushing up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of economies with much weaker growth and lackluster domestic demand?  \par \par \uc2 'This phenomenon is perverse,' says Stephen Jen, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Morgan Stanley in London. He said investors are ' ignoring several dollar positives' including an early resolution of the U.S. presidential election.  \par \par \uc2 Mieczyslaw Karczmar, an economic adviser to Deutsche Bank in New York, says: 'The fall in the dollar is a purely psychological phenomenon based on traders paying attention to the current-account deficit for the first time for years.'  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 Neil King in Washington contributed to this article.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041118e0bi003mm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
