\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar lingers near lows ahead of G20 meeting.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2004\par 08:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The dollar held near lows against the yen and the euro on Wednesday as traders considered what finance officials from the Group of 20 (G20) would do about the dollar's weakness when they meet later in the week.  \par \par \uc2 Large \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were put on pause ahead of the meeting, which will include the Group of Seven developed countries as well as major developing nations and is expected to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues.  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar has been hit the hardest against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , speculation is rife that G20 officials will urge China, which pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, to revalue the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is quite cautious ahead of the G20 at the weekend and any possible China comments," said Luke Waddington, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Royal Bank of Scotland.  \par \par \uc2 Many dealers were betting that Europe would ask Asian nations to accept a weaker dollar, because the euro has risen more than its Asian counterparts, whose gains have been curbed by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The G20 meeting could lead to higher Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Toshiaki Kimura, chief manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Mitsubishi Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "But I'm not sure if this will include the yen since they (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) have not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently."  \par \par \uc2 By 0630 GMT, the dollar bought around 105.35 yen, flat from late New York levels but within sight of a seven-month low of 105.16 hit on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fetched around $1.2980, up from 1.2956 in late U.S. trade and not far from the record high of 1.3006 hit last week. Some analysts said that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were tight due to caution ahead of the financial year-end for many U.S. brokerages and investment banks on Nov. 30, but most market players were confident the dollar's downtrend was firmly in place.  \par \par \uc2 Many traders expect the dollar to fall towards this year's low of 103.40 yen marked in late March. A break through that level would take it to a 4-= year low.  \par \par \uc2 Against the euro, many dealers expected the dollar to tumble as far as $1.35.  \par \par \uc2 DEFICITS WEIGH  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has slid 3.6 percent against the yen over the past month, though its decline versus European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been bigger, falling over five percent against the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 The euro has appreciated over four percent against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Most players expect the underlying weakness in the dollar to stay for as long as the market focuses on the widening U.S. current account deficit and keeps the view that Washington wants a weaker dollar to curb it.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow was due to deliver a speech in London later in the day, but traders said few in the market expected to glean any insight into his views on the dollar's weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Satoru Ogasawara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston, said that any change in the dollar's downtrend would come only if Washington takes clear steps to reduce the deficit or if the Federal Reserve keeps raising interest rates - making dollar assets more attractive.  \par \par \uc2 For clues on future Fed action, the market was awaiting the U.S. consumer price index for October, due at 1330 GMT.  \par \par \uc2 Economists in a Reuters poll forecast an increase of 0.4 percent in the CPI. Excluding energy and food costs, the CPI was seen up 0.1 percent. (Additional reporting by Kazunori Takada).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041118e0bh005f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
