\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-HP lifts Asian tech stocks, weak dlr caps Tokyo.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christopher Kaufman  \par \uc2 687 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2004\par 04:12\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Nov 17 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered above 7-month lows against the yen, pressuring shares in Japanese exporters, but stocks elsewhere in Asia neared a 5-year high as upbeat earnings from Hewlett-Packard boosted tech stocks.  \par \par \uc2 Hewlett-Packard Co, the world's second-biggest computer maker and a component of the Dow Jones Industrial average, shot up 8.54 percent in after-hours trade on a quarterly report that showed it had record revenues in every business and every region.  \par \par \uc2 Strong demand for more than $2.5 billion of shares sold in Chinese, Japanese and Taiwanese initial public offerings also lent support to Asian bourses.  \par \par \uc2 Although the dollar has been hit the hardest against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , speculation is rife that officials from the Group of 20 (G20) countries meeting this weekend will urge China, which pegs its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar, to revalue the yuan.  \par \par \uc2 "The G20 meeting could lead to higher Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Toshiaki Kimura, chief manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  division at Mitsubishi Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "But I'm not sure if this will include the yen since they (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) have not been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently."  \par \par \uc2 By 0225 GMT the dollar stood at 105.42 yen versus 105.36 in late U.S. trade, having slid 3.6 percent over the past month to hit a seven-month low of 105.16 yen on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was steady at around $1.2954 off half a cent from a record high of $1.3006 hit last week.  \par \par \uc2 HONDA REVERSES  \par \par \uc2 Honda Motor Co. Ltd., fell 1.88 percent, leading a general retreat for exporters hurt by the stronger yen, but confidence in Japan's domestic economic outlook curbed selling. The Nikkei average finished morning trade down 0.04 percent at 11,157.03.  \par \par \uc2 MSCI's index of stocks elsewhere in the Asia Pacific rose more than half a percent to its highest level since March 2000, with its technology sub-sector gaining 1.8 percent. MSCI's World Index of stocks was little changed.  \par \par \uc2 Crude oil prices continued their slide ahead of U.S. inventory data due out later in the day that is expected to show stocks rose last week.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. light crude for December delivery was down 25 cents at $45.86 a barrel. Gold prices were close to 16-year highs over $440 an ounce, bid at $439.25.  \par \par \uc2 The HP report boosted Taiwan computer screen makers Au Optronics Corp. and Chi Mei Optoelectronics Corp. more than 6 percent. Both companies supply HP.  \par \par \uc2 The broader Taiwan market gained 1.42 percent, led mostly by the technology sub-index's 2.35 percent gain. South Korean bellwether Samsung Electronics Co. Ltd. jumped 1.32 percent and Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co. (TSMC) rose 1.86 percent following the HP report.  \par \par \uc2 Australian stocks climbed as News Corp. rose 4.5 percent after its admission to the Standard & Poor's 500 index Resource stocks eased.  \par \par \uc2 STRONG IPOS  \par \par \uc2 Shares in Japanese oil explorer INPEX Corp. rose 24 percent on their first day after a $1.5 billion IPO, the third-largest in Japan this year.  \par \par \uc2 A $1.13 billion IPO from China Netcom, the smallest of China's four big telecom carriers and the last to list, also met with strong first-day demand.  \par \par \uc2 In Hong Kong, the stock rose more than 10 percent after rising 14 percent in New York.  \par \par \uc2 State-run Taiwan Cooperative Bank (TCB), the island's second-largest lender, rose the maximum 7 percent in its debut.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks retreated after data showed producer prices skyrocketed on food and energy costs, raising concerns that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might become more aggressive with rate increases in the months ahead.  \par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average fell 0.59 percent to 10,487.65. The broad Standard & Poor's 500 Index was down 0.71 percent to 1,175.43 and the Nasdaq Composite Index slipped 0.74 percent to 2,078.62.  \par \par \uc2 All three indexes posted their largest percentage loss in more than three weeks.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041117e0bh0030g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
