\par\par \b \uc2 WSJ(11/15) Column: For A Few Dollars More  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,468 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2004\par 06:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 WSJ(11/15) Column: For A Few Dollars More  \par \par \uc2 (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL)  \par \par \uc2 By John H. Makin  \par \par \uc2 (Editor's Note: Mr. Makin is a visiting scholar at the American Enterprise Institute.)  \par \par \uc2 Concern abroad about dollar weakness has risen sharply in recent weeks as the trade-weighted dollar has dropped by 6% since Labor Day with two-thirds of the fall coming in the last few weeks. This recent dollar fall continues a weak dollar trend that began at the end of 2002. The trade-weighted dollar fell by about 20% between late 2002 and the winter of this year. During February and March, the dollar's slide was temporarily reversed by a concerted program of dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  led by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  that purchased an extraordinary $130 billion (about a quarter of one year's U.S. current account deficit) in the space of a little over a month. Overall dollar purchases by foreign central banks during the first quarter of this year amounted to over half of the U.S. current account deficit, an unprecedented level of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 As with most episodes of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at trying to fight fundamental pressures for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last winter's Asian stand against further dollar weakness has backfired. After rising by about 7% from its winter lows to a high in May, the dollar was steady during the summer, but has resumed its drop to a level below last winter's lows that prompted the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  surge.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's recent bout of persistent weakness, despite the strong fundamentals of the U.S. economy and Fed tightening, reflects a decision by foreign central banks, especially those in Asia, to reduce their dollar buying and subsequent accommodation of a rising U.S. current account deficit through purchases of U.S. Treasury securities. In the weeks following Oct. 1, foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holdings of treasuries have risen by just $9 billion. That amounts to an annual rate of $95 billion, still substantial, but far below the U.S. annualized $600 billion current account deficit. Some of the reduced purchases of U.S. government and agency securities may reflect a decision to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets while diversifying accumulated reserves into other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like euros, instead of buying U.S. Treasury bills or notes or agency paper. Either way, the dollar falls.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 There are three reasons why foreign central banks have decided to reduce their accommodation of U.S. external deficits. First, and most compelling, is the simple fact that heavy dollar buying by Asian central banks to prevent deflationary appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amounts to a subsidy to U.S. consumption, which, in turn, produces higher and higher U.S. current account deficits while encouraging excess capacity in Asian export industries. The U.S. current account deficit measures the supply of dollars flowing into \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The outcome whereby dollar buying by foreign central banks leads to the need for still more dollar buying has, over the past six months, led the Asian central banks to discover that their dollar-buying efforts are destabilizing. Buying $1 today to prevent dollar depreciation requires buying $1.50 tomorrow to achieve the same goal.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. trade deficit (which constitutes the bulk of the U.S. current account deficit) averaged $51.8 billion per month in the three months ending September. This is up sharply from the $41.4 monthly average in 2003. Little wonder, since as a result of heavy Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar buying, the dollar has only depreciated by 2% against the Japanese yen over the past year and, of course, has depreciated not at all against the Chinese yuan by virtue of the Chinese government's decision to peg its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  adjustment mechanism is not being allowed to operate against the countries (China and Japan) which have the largest ($226 billion annualized through September) trade balance surpluses with the U.S. that are also among the fastest rising.  \par \par \uc2 The problem with China is especially acute. By virtue of the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar, China and the U.S. have the same \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the Federal Reserve -- but need very different monetary policies. If the U.S. were growing at over 9% with inflation having accelerated rapidly to nearly 6% during the past six months, as has occurred in China, the Fed would surely be tightening much faster than at its current 'measured' pace. Based on fundamentals, the yuan needs to appreciate while the dollar needs to depreciate. Preventing this adjustment places a greater burden on other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the euro and the yen, to absorb dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 Beyond the decision by Asian central banks to reduce their dollar purchases, are two other proximate reasons for the enhanced weakness of the dollar over recent weeks. As the cost of oil (priced in dollars) has risen, the temptation to allow one's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to appreciate in order to stabilize the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  price of oil imports has increased. With the euro and the yen rising against the dollar, the price of oil measured in those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has risen less as they have appreciated against the dollar. Central banks and finance ministers in Europe and Asia have said as much.  \par \par \uc2 Finally, the strong re-election victory of President Bush has been taken abroad as a signal, rightly or wrongly, that the U.S. government's dis-saving (budget deficits) will continue to rise, thereby obligating foreign governments to purchase even more dollars and U.S. Treasury securities if they are to accommodate larger U.S. budget deficits while pegging the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The message from the White House on the dollar seems pretty clear. The primary aim of the Bush administration is to undertake fundamental reforms to place Social Security and government health-care programs on a sounder footing by encouraging individuals to save more on their own while working actively with health-care providers to contain rapidly rising costs. Revenue-neutral tax reform aimed at enhancing efficiency by lowering marginal tax rates while closing loopholes is also on the agenda.  \par \par \uc2 While undertaking these important reforms, which will allow markets to work better to enhance the long-run growth path of the U.S. economy, the Bush administration is also prepared to let markets determine exchange rates. That means that European and Japanese pleas for the U.S. government to join in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  efforts to stabilize the dollar will go unheeded. Their choice comes down to either letting the dollar adjust or buying ever-increasing quantities of dollars.  \par \par \uc2 The second message to governments in Europe and Japan, who fear the deflationary impact of further appreciation of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar, is to press the Chinese to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to float upward. China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar has meant that its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated rapidly along with the dollar. Inflation pressures in China have increased while the cost of oil imports for China's heavily oil-dependent export industries has increased as well.  \par \par \uc2 China's domestic economy has also been destabilized by its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg to the dollar. Higher inflation and pegged interest rates leading to potential disintermediation in China's inflexible financial system have already forced Beijing to begin to allow interest rates to rise. Chinese savers don't want to earn 2% on bank deposits while 6% inflation erodes the purchasing power of savings. China's recent move toward greater flexibility of interest rates, hopefully, portends more overall flexibility in China's financial system that will include \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation in order to reduce its need to purchase dollars while simultaneously cushioning China's oil-dependent economy from higher oil prices. Even a moderate 10% or 15% revaluation of China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , perhaps through a transition to a Singapore-style \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket, would ease the pressure for dollar weakness against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the euro, the yen, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Korea and Taiwan.  \par \par \uc2 ---  \par \par \uc2 The reluctance of America's trading partners to allow dollar depreciation, even as the U.S. current account deficit has grown to a point where nearly $2 billion a day must be purchased in order to keep the dollar stable, has created a massive global imbalance. U.S. consumption is being artificially elevated to support rising excess manufacturing capacity in Asia and Europe. While adjustment will involve some transitional pain, the emergence of a virtually zero U.S. saving rate, while Chinese investment rises at a 40% annual rate, is not sustainable. Another year of dollar pegging will mean a U.S current account deficit of over $700 billion and a negative U.S. saving rate. Better to start the adjustment now and let markets determine exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041117e0bh003v0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
