\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Is Japan ready to accept a strong yen?  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Eric Burroughs  \par \uc2 791 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 November 2004\par 08:45\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 16 (Reuters) - The yen's swift rise against the dollar towards a 4-1/2-year peak puts Japan's economy in a squeeze with exports already faltering, but the country may be learning to live with a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen's 6 percent gain in six weeks to nearly 105 per dollar within sight of an April high of 103.40, comes as figures show a marked slowdown in the world's second-largest economy. In the July-September quarter, external demand turned into a net drag on the economy for the first time in two years.  \par \par \uc2 But despite worries yen strength could further dampen exports and turn the slowdown into a contraction, some economists say the outlook is not entirely dire and any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities would probably be limited to containing extreme volatility.  \par \par \uc2 "Any negative impact from a strong yen is being offset by strong growth in corporate profits," said Mamoru Yamazaki, chief economist for Japan at Barclays Capital. "I think exporters are coping with the strong yen."  \par \par \uc2 Both the U.S. and Chinese economies are expanding robustly despite monetary tightening in both countries, offering hope the third-quarter dip in exports was only temporary.  \par \par \uc2 And domestic demand, though tepid, is improving.  \par \par \uc2 A fall of nearly $10 in oil prices from a recent record high is taking some pressure off the economy, even as the dollar's slide lessens the damage by making oil less expensive in yen.  \par \par \uc2 Moreover, Japanese companies are making solid profits, which, along with upbeat news on the U.S. economy, have helped lift the Nikkei stock index 5 percent in the past three weeks despite the yen's rise during that period.  \par \par \uc2 As always with the yen, dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem its gains and limit any potential economic damage may be determined by the speed of the move.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have made clear their anxiety about the pace of the recent gains, a typical precursor to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki warned again on Tuesday against "rapid moves".  \par \par \uc2 Barclays' Yamazaki said a gradual further weakening of the dollar below 105 yen would probably not prompt dollar-buying by Japanese officials, even if it went close to 100 yen. But an abrupt move would trigger action, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Some exporters had anticipated a dollar slide to 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll in September found major exporters like Honda Motor Co. and Sony Corp. had not changed their yen forecasts through the end of fiscal 2004/05 of 105 per dollar. A spokesman for Sony Corp. said on Monday that forecast remained.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  WILD CARDS  \par \par \uc2 Even so, the yen has not sustained a sub-105 level to the dollar since 1994/95, except for a few days earlier this year and a brief period in late 1999/2000.  \par \par \uc2 Already, export orders at manufacturers almost stalled in October and the potential damage from further yen strength cannot be ignored.  \par \par \uc2 When it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , old habits die hard.  \par \par \uc2 For that reason, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists at Banc of America Securities believe the poor performance of Japan's economy in the third quarter means Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which sets \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, "will likely prove less reluctant to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if/as the yen continues to appreciate".  \par \par \uc2 Just a week ago Toyota Motor Corp President Fujio Cho said a further rise in the yen beyond 105 yen to the dollar would be "troublesome" for Japanese businesses.  \par \par \uc2 Peter Morgan, senior economist at HSBC Securities in Tokyo, said the 103-yen level would invite \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  because that is near 103.40, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peaked seven months ago.  \par \par \uc2 But since Japan spent roughly $330 billion trying to fight the dollar's slide between January 2003 and March 2004, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics around the world have become more complicated.  \par \par \uc2 A probable Chinese yuan revaluation in the future poses a problem, since Japanese dollar buying would contradict such a move. Chinese officials maintain that such a policy shift is not imminent, despite market speculation and U.S. insistence.  \par \par \uc2 And any global attempt to correct the massive U.S. current account and budget deficits and their threat to financial and economic stability would require less dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , not only on the part of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  but other Asian central banks.  \par \par \uc2 For now, such a far-reaching accord seems distant.  \par \par \uc2 This week's meeting in Berlin of G20 finance ministers and central bankers from the rich world and the bigger developing countries like China will focus in part on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But some analysts took note that Japan's Tanigaki would be staying at home and sending a deputy instead.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041116e0bg00711\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
