\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar off against yen, takes a breather vs Europe.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 730 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2004\par 16:42\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Recasts, updates prices, adds comments, changes byline and dateline, previous LONDON)  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Nov 15 (Reuters) - The dollar weakened against the yen on Monday, hovering around seven-month lows plumbed overnight, as concerns over U.S. economic policy continued to sour sentiment on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 An almost 2 percent gain in Japan's benchmark Nikkei 225 stock index on Monday also helped lift the yen, which rose against both the dollar and the euro.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was slightly stronger against the European majors as dealers took profits from those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' recent gains and adjusted positions in preparation for a fresh wave of dollar selling.  \par \par \uc2 But worries over high U.S. trade and current account deficits and mounting market speculation Washington might tacitly accept a weaker dollar in order to narrow those gaps mean the dollar is still in the market's crosshairs.  \par \par \uc2 Minutes from the Sept. 21 meeting of the Federal Reserve's monetary policy-setting Open Market Committee released last Friday underscored the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's structural weaknesses.  \par \par \uc2 "The interesting point here is the Fed's acknowledgment that the current position is unsustainable and that adjustments will be required," wrote HSBC \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategists in a research note on Monday. "Such adjustments must surely involve a lower dollar."  \par \par \uc2 In the Fed minutes, the committee discussed the "worrisome further widening" of the deficits and "the significance of wide external deficits and various adjustments that might occur in the process of their return to more sustainable levels."  \par \par \uc2 Fed officials, including Chairman Alan Greenspan, have often voiced such concerns in speeches but this was the first time they had ever been recorded in FOMC minutes, HSBC said.  \par \par \uc2 At 10:00 a.m. EST (1500 GMT) on Monday, the dollar was off 0.2 percent at 105.34 yen. Earlier on Monday, it hit 105.18 yen, its lowest since mid-April.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers said the market was testing the resolve of Japanese monetary authorities to see if they would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling yen to defend 105.00 yen as some sort of line in the sand.  \par \par \uc2 But Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York, reckons Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  will only act if and when the year's low of 103.40 yen comes under threat.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks are that big for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (right now), especially if the stock market continues to rise," she said.  \par \par \uc2 OIL HEADS LOWER  \par \par \uc2 Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials has helped slow the ascent in the euro, which hit a lifetime high of $1.3005 last Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 ECB board member Tommaso Padoa-Schioppa said on Monday he doesn't believe the market has a one-way bet on the euro, and echoed ECB president Jean-Claude Trichet's comments that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent spike up was "brutal" and unwelcome.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the rhetoric from ECB officials is going to get even more extreme," said TJ Marta, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at RBC Capital Markets in New York.  \par \par \uc2 This, and the steady decline in oil prices to a two-month low comfortably below $50 a barrel, is helping relieve some of the selling pressure on the dollar on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's in dollar-selling mode ... but crude oil is preventing people from outright bashing the dollar," said Jeremy Fand, senior trader at WestLB in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was a bit weaker at $1.2952, as was sterling, at $1.8521.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was up 0.4 percent at 1.1764 Swiss francs.  \par \par \uc2 Gold, often seen as a proxy for the euro, hit a 16-year high on Monday of $440 an ounce, indicating that dollar sentiment remains weak, and the dollar index hit a nine-year low earlier on Monday of 83.60.  \par \par \uc2 While the market appears to be taking a breather on Monday, dealers have plenty coming up in the week to look forward to.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. capital flows data for September from the Treasury on Tuesday, October U.S. inflation and industrial production data on Wednesday, and the G20 meeting in Berlin at the weekend could all give the dollar direction in the next few sessions.  \par \par \uc2 The New York Fed's Empire State manufacturing index ticked up in November to 19.8 from 17.4 in October, but this had little impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041115e0bf009fg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
