\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hits low vs yen as U.S. seen wanting weakness.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kininmont  \par \uc2 727 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 November 2004\par 04:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 15 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a seven-month low against the yen and was within striking distance of a record trough versus the euro on Monday due to mounting market speculation that Washington was happy to see its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken.  \par \par \uc2 Persistent worries about the giant U.S.need to correct its giant current account and budget deficits has prompted traders to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  even lower, despite upbeat U.S. economic data and the Federal Reserve's commitment to hiking interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 That has led market players to suspect that the U.S., despite its repeated support for a "strong" dollar, in fact would prefer to see it weaken as a means of fixing the deficits.  \par \par \uc2 Even verbal warnings from European and Japanese officials about sharp \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements and volatility - sometimes a precursor to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - have only temporarily stemmed the dollar's plunge much.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's role in funding those deficits stepped into the spotlight with the yen's latest gains against the dollar, as traders have speculated that repatriation of coupon payments on U.s. Treasuries may be lifting the yen. But some dealers in Tokyo played down the role of any repatration flows.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar failed to find support from solid economic data released on Friday that suggested the pace of U.S. growth was accelerating, as worries persisted about the huge U.S. deficits.  \par \par \uc2 "Listening to the comments of officials in major countries, the U.S. current account deficit is causing more and more worries these days," said Tohru Sasaki, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase.  \par \par \uc2 "So if the U.S. authorities are thinking about such things (keeping the dollar weak), I am not so surprised."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell as far as around 105.20 yen, the lowest mark since mid-April. By 0050 GMT it fetched around 105.25, versus 105.57 in late New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  fell sharply on Friday on expected repatriation flows into the yen triggered by some $68 billion of U.S. Treasury debt and coupon payments that are due to mature on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 However, analysts in Tokyo said they did not expect large flows back into Japan as Japanese investors usually reinvest into U.S. treasuries.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese accounts are the biggest single group holding U.S. Treasuries, owning some $722 billion as of the end of August, with the Bank of Japan holding most of that.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fetched around $1.2980 , near the all-time high of $1.3006 struck last week. It was slightly up from the level in late trade in New York, where it rose around 0.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was also dented on Friday after minutes of the Federal Reserve's policy-setting committee meeting on Sept. 21 expressed concern about the massive U.S. trade gap.  \par \par \uc2 It initially edged higher against the euro after a stronger-than-expected University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey, but soon reversed course.  \par \par \uc2 Robust U.S. retail sales suggested the economy was growing strongly in the fourth quarter, giving the Fed leeway to continue raising interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 VERBAL \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 The upbeat economic indicators did not alter market concerns about the gaping trade gap and perceptions that Washington sees a weaker dollar as a means to correct the deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei Financial Daily reported on Friday that Washington was set to accept a weaker dollar to cushion the economic impact from tighter monetary and fiscal policies and reduce the current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 European central bankers stepped up their rhetoric last week against the euro's sharp rise against the dollar, which is seen undermining an export-led recovery that is already looking fragile.  \par \par \uc2 In the most recent salvo, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Vice President Lucas Papademos reiterated in Tokyo on Friday that disorderly movements and excess volatility in exchange rates were not desirable.  \par \par \uc2 The strength of the euro will be a key talking point for euro zone finance ministers when they hold a monthly meeting later in the day Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Amid a lack of Japanese data this week, traders were looking to September's U.S. net capital flows data issued by the Treasury Department, although many analysts do not expect the report to spur much dollar buying.  \par \par \uc2 In August, net inflows totaled $59 billion.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041115e0bf0012y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
