\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slides versus yen, current account weighs.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Carolyn Cohn  \par \uc2 757 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2004\par 12:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds euro zone data, ECB comments, Japan article)  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The dollar fell two thirds of a percent against the yen to within sight of seven-month lows and edged towards recent record lows against the euro on Friday as worries persisted about the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has lost as much as five percent against the yen and six percent against the euro since early October as markets fret the U.S. will fail to attract sufficient investor flows to plug its huge current account gap.  \par \par \uc2 Recent speculation in U.S. and Japanese newspapers that the U.S. administration will tolerate a weaker dollar was boosting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday, while verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from euro zone policymakers kept the euro in check.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is drifting downwards. There is an inclination to sell dollars - the current account deficit is the underlying concern. We need to see a big upward surprise in today's U.S. data to change the sentiment," said Daragh Maher, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Calyon.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. retail sales data for October at 1330 GMT are forecast to show a rise of 0.2 percent, from a 1.5 percent gain in September.  \par \par \uc2 The University of Michigan preliminary sentiment survey for November is due at 1445 GMT and forecast to show a rise to 93.0 from 91.7 in October.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.2920 at 1115 GMT, slightly up from late U.S. trade, after hitting a record high of $1.3005 on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 84 against an index of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a little above recent nine-year lows.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell to 105.84 yen, just over half a yen away from seven-month lows.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was finding support from a report in the Nikkei Financial Daily that the U.S. administration was set to accept a weaker dollar to cushion the economic impact from tighter monetary and fiscal policies and reduce the current account gap.  \par \par \uc2 The front-page article gave no sources for its conclusion but said the dollar would need to fall by up to 20-30 percent in order to halve the ratio of the U.S. current account deficit to gross domestic product solely via \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said there was also some talk of dollar sales against the yen ahead of U.S. Treasury coupon repayments on Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Falling oil prices were also seen helping the yen, as Japan imports all of its oil.  \par \par \uc2 EURO TALK  \par \par \uc2 The euro was capped as a recent chorus of comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers helped the dollar's cause.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday he stood by a statement he made on Monday in which he described recent euro/dollar movements as "brutal", while colleagues expressed concerns about the pace of the euro's recent gains.  \par \par \uc2 Similar "brutal" remarks by Trichet in January took more than three percent off the value of the euro in less than a week, while this week the euro was trading only a little below Monday's opening levels.  \par \par \uc2 "We have had a bit of profit-taking, but Trichet's comments have had less impact on the euro than in January," said Trevor Dinmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deutsche Bank.  \par \par \uc2 ECB Vice President Lucas Papademos reiterated in Tokyo on Friday that excess volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets was not desirable.  \par \par \uc2 ECB Executive Board member Gertrude Tumpel-Gugerell said the risk from high oil prices to euro zone growth and inflation has grown.  \par \par \uc2 A strong euro buffers the euro zone from high oil costs, as the euro zone is reliant on oil imports.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone growth slumped to its slowest pace for more than a year in the third quarter, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets ignored the data.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN GROWTH  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, the yen quickly reversed losses made after news the economy grew a real 0.1 percent in July-September from the previous quarter. That was below economists' forecasts for growth of 0.5 percent.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was also finding support from speculation over whether and when China would alter its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 China's yuan trades in a narrow range at around 8.28 to the dollar, but it is widely believed to be undervalued and helping Chinese exporters.  \par \par \uc2 Beijing has come under pressure, particularly from Washington, to adopt a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  system.  \par \par \uc2 Such a revaluation would likely support the yen and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041112e0bc00d72\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
