\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: You Say Volatility, We Say Orderly Decline  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,102 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2004\par 22:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Grainne McCarthy \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Judging by candid comments from the U.S. Treasury on Friday, one man's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  volatility is another man's orderly decline.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Briefing reporters ahead of a meeting next week of Group of 20 finance ministers and central bankers in Berlin, a Treasury official said financial markets, including \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, have been orderly.  \par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are operating very well, as all markets are," the official told reporters on the condition he not be named. "You have general measures of volatility down in all markets - that's good - risks are low so interest rates can be low." The official declined to comment on the direction or level of the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 He didn't need need to. For \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the message was clear: the U.S. isn't unhappy with current exchange rate levels. "I think it is a For Sale sign," on the dollar, said Greg Anderson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN Amro in Chicago. He said the message was essentially a reaffirmation of the Group of Seven leading industrial nations' dislike of "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates."  \par \par \uc2 But in the wake of a string of alarmed remarks from policymakers in Japan and the euro zone about the dollar's slide, the Treasury official was also making another important point. "The Europeans and Japanese think excessive volatility means movement beyond levels that make them uncomfortable," said Anderson. "He's saying the U.S. definition of volatility is mathematical."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, while the euro has been on a charge higher - it's gained about 5% against the dollar since the beginning of October and briefly pierced the $1.30 barrier this week for the first time ever - volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  option markets remains a little below five-year averages, as Anderson noted.  \par \par \uc2 Likewise, although the dollar's broad weakening has grabbed attention, it's been gradual and nowhere near the kind of sudden lurches seen during emerging market crises or market seizures in 1998 that followed Russia's debt default and the collapse of hedge fund Long Term Capital Management.  \par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, orderly is a coded word that carries a lot of weight. In saying "I think there's been a great deal of orderliness in financial markets", the Treasury official flagged the fact that the U.S. isn't unhappy with how market participants are pushing exchange rates.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      No \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 It's a view that's apparently not shared on the other side of the Atlantic.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This week alone, a chorus of policy makers in Europe have expressed growing concern about the damage the stronger euro could do to the struggling euro zone economy. Most notably, European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Jean-Claude Trichet weighed in on Monday with very targeted language, saying "The recent moves which tend to be brutal on the exchange markets between the euro and the U.S dollar are not welcome from the standpoint of the ECB."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Others both in and outside the ECB have termed recent moves as too rapid. Nout Wellink, ECB governing council member and Dutch \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  president, even borrowed some of the coded G7 language, saying the euro's rise "has gone rather fast," which he said was worrying because "excess volatility creates uncertainty."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comments, along with the continued steady flow of verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's policy makers, raised the question of whether the Japanese government, or even the ECB, might be tempted to put their money where their collective mouth is to push their own \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  down against the dollar. The Treasury official's comments, in particular his description of markets as orderly, put paid to that theory, certainly when it comes to whether the U.S. would take part in any such foray into the markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This is a very important phrase," said Andy Busch, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Harris Nesbitt in Chicago. Orderly "is the tagline, the buzz word that central bankers use when they seek to provide justification of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. The U.S. is therefore making it very clear to their partners that they are not going to engage in any \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's not to say that the ECB or \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  couldn't act on their own accounts, but the chances of such an attempt to strengthen the dollar having any success would be greatly limited if the U.S. wasn't involved.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the large number of speculators who have been heavily short dollars in the past few weeks, that's yet further proof that despite the lip service the Bush administration might pay to the so-called strong dollar policy, it truly no longer exists. A weaker \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  helps U.S. manufacturers because it hands them a competitive edge on global markets. And as several Federal Reserve officials have pointed out, a declining dollar should help make a dent in the massive deficit in the U.S. current account.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the Treasury official implicitly alluded to, what's crucial in not upsetting this delicate apple cart is the pace of a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move. If foreign investors really buy into the idea that the U.S. doesn't mind a gradually declining dollar, that could have far-reaching consequences for investment in U.S. markets at a time when the current account and federal budget deficits are very high.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin had some thoughts on the subject this week.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If markets begin to fear long-term fiscal disarray and if foreign providers of the capital inflows upon which we have now become so enormously dependent share this fear and also develop a concern about our \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , then the markets may begin to demand sharply higher interest rates on long-term debt and possibly even create conditions of serious disruptions in our financial markets, with all the problems that that can lead to for our economy," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rubin, the godfather of the strong dollar policy while in the Clinton administration, clearly showed where he would stand now. "If I were still at Treasury, I would still be an advocate of a strong dollar policy," he said, in remarks at a dinner in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Grainne McCarthy is Assistant Managing Editor for fixed income, macroeconomics and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  news. Grainne was previously Indonesia bureau chief and also reported for Dow Jones Newswires in Belgium).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Grainne McCarthy; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2381; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Laurence Norman contributed to this article). [ 11-12-04 1604ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041112e0bc00038\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
