\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar inches up vs yen after soft Japan GDP.  \b0\par\par\uc2 340 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2004\par 01:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 12 (Reuters) - The dollar edged up against the yen on Friday after disappointing growth data for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and was supported against the euro by ongoing verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from European central bankers.   \par \par \uc2  The yen gave up early gains after traders used figures showing Japan's economy grew a real 0.1 percent in July-September from the previous quarter as a sign to sell the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Economists had expected growth of 0.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2  "The market had been anticipating these figures, and short dollar positions had been piling up, so it was inevitable that a weaker figure would prompt yen selling," said Hideaki Furumaya, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Trust and Custody Services.   \par \par \uc2  "With figures like these, it's possible that the risks of recent yen strength have subsided a little."   \par \par \uc2  As of 0049 GMT, the dollar bought around 106.70 yen compared with 106.63 yen in quiet trading in New York, where movements were thinned due to the Veterans Day holiday.   \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to the day's low around 106.80 yen shortly after the gross domestic product (GDP) data was announced.   \par \par \uc2  Against the euro, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traded at $1.2905, essentially flat from late U.S. trading.   \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the week, the euro climbed to a record high of $1.3006.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar has plunged around six percent in the past month, chased downward by market concerns about U.S. deficits.   \par \par \uc2  While market players expect the dollar to stay on a downtrend, some analysts said a chorus of comments from European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policymakers was helping to prop up the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet said on Thursday that he stood by a statement he made earlier in the week in which he described recent euro/dollar movements as "brutal".   \par \par \uc2  A number of his colleagues on the ECB Governing Council, including Nout Wellink and Nicholas Garganas the same day expressed concerns about the speed at which the euro has been gaining against the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041112e0bc0010d\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
