\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Mixed Verbal Messages Are Frying The Dollar  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven Vames  \par \uc2 898 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2004\par 06:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--If too many cooks can spoil the broth, then too many mixed messages from global monetary officials can certainly spoil the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The huge speculative selloff that's plagued the dollar in recent weeks has run against the backdrop of U.S. economic growth that is faster and more surefooted than Europe and Japan's, unchanged prospects for higher U.S. interest rates and the end of a presidential election that has been met with inflows of foreign money into U.S. markets.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, that's all been theoretically positive news for the dollar and perhaps the only concrete argument for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the U.S. current account deficit - something that continues to get fully funded by foreign investors and that the market has been keenly aware of for some time.  \par \par \uc2 So why all of the desire to push the dollar down? It's in large part because global monetary officials aren't doing much to stem the buck's losses.  \par \par \uc2 In recent weeks, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets have watched as European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said at some points that a stronger euro doesn't bug them so much, while at other times called the dollar's drop 'brutal' and 'unwelcome.'  \par \par \uc2 Then back to the other extreme: on Wednesday, there was unconfirmed speculation swirling in the market that the ECB actually has levels targeted for the euro - significantly stronger than where it is now - at which it would get hot under the collar. A euro at $1.30 or $1.35 is tolerable, the talk goes, but at $1.40 it would start to really irritate the ECB. The euro briefly jumped above the $1.30 barrier earlier Wednesday, hitting a fresh record of $1.3005 before slipping back toward $1.2874 as technical factors weighed on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Then another U-turn, when Italian Economy Minister Domenico Siniscalco said the ECB was mulling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stem the euro's sharp rise. 'We're talking about a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not a unilateral one, which would be ineffective,' Siniscalco said during a press conference, MF-Dow Jones reported.  \par \par \uc2 Even German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder and French President Jacques Chirac have weighed in on the euro, suggesting there are few rules about who should, and shouldn't, be jawboning the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par       Fed, Treasury Different Lines \par  \par   Meanwhile the Bush Administration has maintained its apparent preference for a 'strong dollar' policy but has been widely seen as a secret advocate of a weaker dollar, since it is theoretically good for U.S. exporters. \par   At the same time, a handful of Federal Reserve officials have suggested that logically, the dollar has to go lower to adjust to the giant imbalance between imports, exports, and foreign investment into the U.S., known as the current account deficit. But there is ultimately little consensus on how much the dollar would have to fall to make a dent, particularly with the moving targets of oil prices and relative economics. \par   Japanese officials, meanwhile, have been more unified in their hawkish message that they will act if they think markets are acting irrationally. When they speak, markets listen, since they have been one of the most lethal users of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . But even their message has been diluted by questions over whether \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would actually \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  given the improved footing of its economy and staunch rhetoric coming out of U.S. and Group of Seven countries recently advocating 'flexible' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regimes. \par  \par     Messages Garbled \par  \par   With so many messages, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market might just be hearing white noise, giving traders the green light to do what they want to do: take the dollar lower because it's the easiest and most profitable trade at this point. \par   \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets are, by and large, hugely speculative. Though long-term movements generally follow global political and economic trends, the week-to-week noise in between those trends is squarely in the hands of traders no different from those that engage in short-term trading of stocks. \par   Stocks, of course have a speculative element, but they also have discrete numerical benchmarks, such as price-to-earnings ratios and uniform quarterly earnings reports. In contrast, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders have only the information of economic trends, which can be very vague from a trees-within-the-forest perspective. \par   So \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets widely depend on monetary officials - typically global central bankers and U.S. Treasury officials - to tell them when they are being rational and irrational. Some monetary authorities, Japan's in particular, have traditionally done so by muscling the mass of their huge reserves squarely into the face of speculators. \par   But more often, the tongues of central bankers can more-or-less be heard in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. That is, if they can convey a unified message. When they can't all hit the same chord, it's essentially a green light for speculators to do what they want, which is exactly what the current fits and swings in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets have reflected. \par  \par   (Steven Vames covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in New York. He as also covered equities in Los Angeles and the Treasurys market and fixed income industry in New York.) \par  \par    -Steven Vames; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-5063; steven.vames@dowjones.com \par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -0-   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041111e0bb003jw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
