\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Little Changed After Trichet, Pre-Fed -2-  \b0\par\par\uc2 331 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 07:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Some market watchers say the euro may not breach $1.3000 and the dollar may not fall below Y105.00 due to concern that the ECB and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  would step up verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem their respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' strength.  \par \par \uc2 A stronger \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is widely considered a drag on exports because it makes products less competitive in overseas markets.  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, Japanese finance minister Sadakazu Tanigaki chimed in with his own warning on Tuesday. "Our fundamental stance is that when markets don't stably reflect fundamentals, we will act resolutely" against such moves, Tanigaki said at a regular press conference. Some traders said Tanigaki's comments were stronger than recent statements by other MOF officials about the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, although the yen showed no reaction.  \par \par \uc2 Corporate Japan also offered up its opinion of the value of the yen. Toyota Motor Corp. (7203.TO) President Fujio Cho said Tuesday any further rise in the yen against the dollar from the Y105.00 level would greatly affect the automaker and cloud the outlook for the Japanese economy.  \par \par \uc2 Still, few analysts say that actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from either the ECB or the MOF is imminent.  \par \par \uc2 If the ECB enters the market, it may do so only after the euro has reached the high $1.3000s and in order to defend $1.4000, according to Stephen Jen, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Morgan Stanley. But "the market now appears very determined to take the dollar lower," Jen added in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 The MOF won't take action unless the dollar falls well below Y105.00 and Japanese stock prices plunge as a result, said Credit Suisse First Boston strategist Satoru Ogasawara. However, "the turning point would be whether it's an 'orderly dollar fall' or a 'dollar free fall'," he added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Hugh Lawson \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-11-04 0644GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041109e0b9000dm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
