\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Little Changed After Trichet, Before Fed  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stanley White  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 953 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 06:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar was little changed against the euro and the yen in Asia Tuesday as a warning against euro strength from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  gave investors an excuse to unwind dollar-shorts and square positions before a Federal Reserve meeting.  \par \par \uc2 ECB President Jean-Claude Trichet said Monday in Switzerland, "the recent moves which tend to be brutal on the exchange markets between the euro and the U.S. dollar are not welcome from the standpoint of the ECB." He was speaking after a meeting at the Bank for International Settlements with G10 and emerging market nations.  \par \par \uc2 Trichet's comment, which many traders labeled "strong" verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , helped the dollar extend its overnight gains versus the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  early into the Asian session.  \par \par \uc2 The euro dipped to an intraday low of $1.2890 as some investors tried to trigger stop-loss orders, traders said. But the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then bounced back to late New York levels on buying from overseas hedge funds and Asian central banks, traders added.  \par \par \uc2 The euro's movement against the dollar also encouraged investors to unwind dollar-short positions against the yen. However, bids from Japanese exporters kept the greenback in a tight range for most of the session.  \par \par \uc2 "People who don't have positions are not going to build them now," said Jun Kitazawa, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at BBH Investor Services. "But the dollar still seems to be very weak, and people are looking for a place to sell dollars."  \par \par \uc2 The fact that overseas investors were willing to buy the euro on dips shows that the recent wave of dollar-negative sentiment due to worries about the U.S. current account and budget deficits hasn't changed, Kitazawa also said.  \par \par \uc2 The next opportunity for investors may come with a Fed meeting and the release of September U.S. trade data on Wednesday. The Fed is widely expected to raise interest rates by 25 basis points to 2.00%, but investors will pore over the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's policy statement for further hints on the pace of future interest rate hikes. The U.S. trade deficit is expected to have narrowed to $53.5 billion in September from $54 billion the month before, according to economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires.  \par \par \uc2 If both events are positive for the dollar, the euro could fall to $1.2800 and the greenback could reach Y106.00, according to Tohru Sasaki, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan. But if the Fed and the trade data were to disappoint, the euro could test $1.3000 and the dollar cold fall to Y105.00, Sasaki said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High \par    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              105.58-61  105.51-56  +0.07  105.67  105.46   -1.64 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2910-20  1.2918-19  -0.06  1.2928  1.2891   +2.49 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.8538-44  1.8562-71  -0.13  1.8577  1.8514   +3.78 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.1822-26  1.1811-17  +0.09  1.1843  1.1810   -4.63 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.1947-53  1.1944-50  +0.03  1.1950  1.1933   -8.05 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.7564-69  0.7576-79  -0.16  0.7577  0.7556   +0.42 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.6915-25  0.6915-19   0.00  0.6923  0.6908   +5.57 \par USD/DEM Deutsche Mark    2.2150-75  2.2150-75   0.00  2.2157  2.2160   -0.11 \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-11-04 0536GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some market watchers say the euro may not breach $1.3000 and the dollar may not fall below Y105.00 due to concern that the ECB and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would step up verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem their respective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ' strength.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A stronger \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is widely considered a drag on exports because it makes products less competitive in overseas markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Indeed, Japanese finance minister Sadakazu Tanigaki chimed in with his own warning on Tuesday. "Our fundamental stance is that when markets don't stably reflect fundamentals, we will act resolutely" against such moves, Tanigaki said at a regular press conference. Some traders said Tanigaki's comments were stronger than recent statements by other MOF officials about the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, although the yen showed no reaction.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corporate Japan also offered up its opinion of the value of the yen. Toyota Motor Corp. (7203.TO) President Fujio Cho said Tuesday any further rise in the yen against the dollar from the Y105.00 level would greatly affect the automaker and cloud the outlook for the Japanese economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, few analysts say that actual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from either the ECB or the MOF is imminent.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the ECB enters the market, it may do so only after the euro has reached the high $1.3000s and in order to defend $1.4000, according to Stephen Jen, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  research at Morgan Stanley. But "the market now appears very determined to take the dollar lower," Jen added in a research note.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The MOF won't take action unless the dollar falls well below Y105.00 and Japanese stock prices plunge as a result, said Credit Suisse First Boston strategist Satoru Ogasawara. However, "the turning point would be whether it's an 'orderly dollar fall' or a 'dollar free fall'," he added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Hugh Lawson \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 09-11-04 0644GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041109e0b90009l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
