\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 1-Europe's policy makers fret over dollar slide.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Sumeet Desai  \par \uc2 617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 November 2004\par 16:53\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 9 (Reuters) - European policy makers voiced growing fears on Tuesday that the dollar's slide against Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may jeopardise global recovery.  \par \par \uc2 As the dollar traded near record lows against the euro, the European Commission warned that sharp exchange rate moves were bad for growth. It said the issue was certain to figure at next week's meetings of euro zone finance ministers.  \par \par \uc2 In Britain, finance minister Gordon Brown told business leaders the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gyrations posed yet another challenge to the world economy as it also grappled with high oil prices, trade imbalances and Middle East instability.  \par \par \uc2 "All of you are having to cope with the doubling of oil prices, rising world commodity prices, uncertainty in the Middle East, large current account imbalances between Europe, Asia and America and the changing values of the dollar, euro and pound," he told a business conference in Birmingham, central England.  \par \par \uc2 "And thus (we have) uncertainties about the strength and durability of the world recovery," he added.  \par \par \uc2 His French counterpart Nicolas Sarkozy, also in Birmingham for the same conference, blamed the United States for its huge deficits which are helping drive the dollar down.  \par \par \uc2 Asked by reporters if he was worried by the dollar's fall, Sarkozy replied: "It's not a question of anxiety, it is like I said in Italy yesterday, it's a problem with American deficits and our American friends should reduce it."  \par \par \uc2 He had also seemingly criticised the U.S. over its dollar policy in Rome on Monday, saying the Group of Seven finance ministers' statement made in Florida in February should remain valid.  \par \par \uc2 "The Europeans and Americans jointly signed the Boca Raton G7 statement and we believe that the statement remains valid and I think the United States must remember it," he said then.  \par \par \uc2 The Florida G7 statement said that "excess volatility and disorderly movements in exchange rates are undesirable for economic growth."  \par \par \uc2 German Finance Minister Hans Eichel said last week the G7 would discuss the euro/dollar exchange rate if Europe's single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to strengthen at recent rates.  \par \par \uc2 While no formal meeting of G7 finance ministers is planned for the rest of the year, ministers and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors of the rich nations' club will attend the G20 group of developed and emerging market economies in Berlin on November 19 and 20.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow travels to Europe early next week.  \par \par \uc2 ECB NOT HAPPY  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit a record high against the dollar on Monday of $1.2985 but has since fallen below $1.29 following a survey showing German business confidence slumping to its worst level in two years as the strength of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hit exports.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement said in Berlin that the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) would pay close attention to moves in the exchange rate and would be ready to act if necessary.  \par \par \uc2 The ECB is certainly not happy.  \par \par \uc2 "It would not be desirable that the appreciation of the euro should continue, still less that it should accelerate," ECB Governing Council member Guy Quaden told a Belgian newspaper on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. must reduce its budget and current account deficit as quickly as possible, European countries must raise their growth potential and Asian countries ease their exchange rate policy," he was quoted as saying.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen from appreciating, also hinted it would be ready to act if the dollar slide continued.  \par \par \uc2 "Our basic stance is to act in a timely way and decisively if movements deviate from fundamentals," Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said in Tokyo.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041109e0b900ao5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
