\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slides to fresh record low versus euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 568 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2004\par 12:32\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 8 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a fresh record low against the euro and a nine-year low on a trade-weighted index on Monday as investors continued to shun the greenback on worries over the United States's bloated deficits.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar weakened as far as $1.2985 to the euro in early trade and analysts said it was only a matter of time before it reached the psychological $1.30 level.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. has a current account deficit, a budget deficit and a president who appears unconcerned about dollar weakness," said Shahab Jalinoos, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO. "No one can see any reason to buy the dollar at the moment."  \par \par \uc2 The latest bout of dollar weakness began last Wednesday as investors took the view that the administration of re-elected President George W. Bush would do little to alleviate the twin U.S. deficits.  \par \par \uc2 The budget deficit is about $427 billion, or 3.7 percent of gross domestic product, while the current account, the broadest measure of trade, hit a record $166.18 billion shortfall in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's weakness was broad based with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting a nine-year low against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  below 83.80, a 12-year low against the Canadian dollar and multi-month lows against sterling and the yen.  \par \par \uc2 By 1100 GMT, the dollar was trading at $1.2960 to the euro and 105.46 yen, just above session lows. European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (ECB) President Jean-Claude Trichet is expected to hold a news conference later, possibly around 1230 GMT, and the market is keen to hear whether he voices concern over recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves.  \par \par \uc2 TOLERANCE THRESHOLD  \par \par \uc2 Top central bankers meeting in Basel said on Sunday they were keeping a wary eye on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts said Japanese and euro zone policymakers were unlikely to step into the market to stop the dollar's fall at this stage.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro/dollar's break of $1.30 is only a matter of time," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas. "It's just a question of momentum."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily on the foreign exchanges, selling about 35 trillion yen ($332.2 billion) in 2003 through to March this year. But analysts say Tokyo is unlikely to repeat such a large scale campaign given the Japanese economy is now in better shape.  \par \par \uc2 Euro zone policymakers have also sounded fairly relaxed about recent exchange rate moves, noting a weaker dollar helps to combat inflation and cushion the impact of high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 ECB Chief Economist Otmar Issing declined on Monday to comment on the euro's exchange rate, while Germany's DIHK chambers of trade and industry said the euro would not threaten Germany's 2005 growth prospects unless it rose above $1.30 for a "lasting" period.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's best chance of a rebound in the near term is after the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting on Wednesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The market has already priced in a quarter-point U.S. rate rise this month but is less certain about a hike in December. If the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicates it will raise rates again next month, that could help the dollar, as higher rates are likely to draw more foreign capital into the United States.   