\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar wilts to record lows vs euro.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Hideyuki Sano  \par \uc2 607 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2004\par 08:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 8 (Reuters) - The dollar fell to a record low against the euro and to multi-month troughs versus other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday, after strong U.S. employment data last week failed to dispel worries about the bloated U.S. current account deficit. The dollar hit an all-time low of around $1.2986 per euro in late Tokyo trade, after it marked a low of $1.2973 in New York on Friday despite figures showing a surprising increase in the number of new U.S. jobs created in October.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's moves since Friday confirm that the market is focusing on structural problems rather than cyclical improvements in the U.S. economy," said Kikuko Takeda, market analyst at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 At 0633 GMT, the euro fetched around $1.2975, about five percent above its level just a month ago.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts painted a dim picture for the dollar, saying that a fall to $1.30 was inevitable.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro/dollar's break of $1.30 is only a matter of time," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "It's just a question of momentum."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was weaker against other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It hit a seven-month low of 105.33 yen and stood at 105.50 yen, slightly off late U.S levels around 105.60.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it would be a good idea to sell the dollar until it reaches this year's low around 103 yen," said Tokyo-Mitsubishi's Takeda.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar also hit an eight-year low around 1.1766 Swiss francs, while the British pound hit a four-month high of $1.8575.  \par \par \uc2 DEFICIT DOGS DOLLAR  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's broad weakness stemmed from speculation that it needs to be much weaker or else the ballooning U.S. trade gap with the rest of the world could blow out to unmanageable levels.  \par \par \uc2 The current account - the broadest trade measure since it adds investment flows - hit a record $166.18 billion shortfall in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Worries about the deficit cancelled out signs of an improvement in the U.S. job market.  \par \par \uc2 Friday's data showed non-farm payrolls rose by 337,000 in October - double market forecasts.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also said that Japan and euro-zone policy makers were unlikely to step into the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to stop the dollar's fall at this stage.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily in the market, selling about 35 trillion yen ($332.2 billion) in 2003 through to March 2004. But analysts say Tokyo is unlikely to repeat such a large scale campaign given the Japanese economy is now in much better shape.  \par \par \uc2 Speculation that the European authorities could \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  was kept in check on Monday as traders had been pacified by comments from German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder late last week.  \par \par \uc2 Schroeder had said that while he found recent euro/dollar levels "worrying", German exporters could manage at current rates.  \par \par \uc2 For now, the dollar's best chance of a rebound in the near term is after the U.S. Federal Reserve's policy meeting on Wednesday, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The market has already priced in a rate rise of 25 basis points. But if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicates that it might raise rates in December, that could help the dollar, as higher rates are seen helping to draw more global capital to the United States.  \par \par \uc2 "If the Fed's statement indicates no rate hike in December, there will be another wave of dollar selling," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities. ($1=105.36 yen) (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041108e0b8009hw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
