\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar hits record low vs euro, slumps vs yen.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Naomi Tajitsu  \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 November 2004\par 04:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 8 (Reuters) - The dollar hit a record low against the euro and a multi-month trough versus the yen on Monday on worries about bloated deficits in the United States, and traders said the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely suffer further losses.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar slumped to an all-time low of around $1.2975 per euro in early Tokyo trade, after it marked a low of $1.2973 in New York on Friday despite data showing a surprising increase in the number of new U.S. jobs created in October.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar slid to a fresh 6-= month low of 105.33 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Many analysts painted a dim picture for the dollar, adding that a fall to $1.30 against the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was inevitable.  \par \par \uc2 "The euro/dollar's break of $1.30 is only a matter of time," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "It's just a question of momentum."  \par \par \uc2 By 0313 GMT, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had regained its footing to trade around $1.2965 per euro, near late New York levels.  \par \par \uc2 It fetched 105.43 yen versus 105.58.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bought 1.1788 Swiss francs, within sight of eight-year lows hit on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 A rise in the price of gold to 16-year highs was also seen adding pressure to the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 An expected rise in U.S. interest rates is unlikely to give the dollar much relief from blows it has received in recent weeks, analysts said.  \par \par \uc2 The market has priced in a rate rise of 25 basis points after the Federal Reserve ends its two-day policy meeting on Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 That would take the benchmark rate to 2.0 percent and mark the fourth rise this year.  \par \par \uc2 Still, U.S. interest rates and Friday's data showing U.S. non-farm payrolls rose by 337,000 in October - double market forecasts - were taking a back seat to concerns about the U.S. twin deficits, which have been a key driver of dollar weakness.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. budget deficit is about $427 billion, or 3.7 percent of gross domestic product, while the current account - the broadest trade measure since it adds investment flows - hit a record $166.18 billion shortfall in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 JAPAN GROWTH FIGURES  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has fallen 5.3 percent against the yen in the past month.  \par \par \uc2 Traders have expressed some concerns that Japan might step into the market to stem the rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and protect an export-led economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 However, some analysts said that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would not likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  at present even if upcoming Japanese gross domestic product (GDP) figures for the third quarter offer an unexpected boost to the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "One of the preconditions for expecting the MOF (\b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to stand back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a continuing domestic recovery, and we have heard from domestic officials that recent soft data is temporary," said Fink at Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "So I think this is one reason for (the GDP) data to be viewed as fairly important."  \par \par \uc2 The GDP data for July-September is due on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed that the Japanese economy grew by 0.5 percent, slightly stronger than the 0.3 percent growth seen in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts said that speculation about market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the European authorities was in check on Monday as traders had been pacified by comments from German Chancellor Gerhard Schroeder late last week.  \par \par \uc2 Schroeder had said that while he found recent euro/dollar levels "worrying", German exporters could manage at current rates.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041108e0b8004ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
