\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar steadies near 9-year low before jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 703 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2004\par 14:11\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 5 (Reuters) - The dollar hovered near a 9-year low on a trade-weighted index as investors waited to see if Friday's U.S. jobs report would spell more trouble for the greenback or give it a reprieve.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has come under fire in the last two days as the market has switched its attention away from the U.S. election and back to the country's structural problems, particularly the U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing a pause before the payrolls but generally I think people remain happy to play the trend and drive the dollar lower," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. jobs data at 1330 GMT will be closely watched for clues on the sustainability of the U.S. recovery and the likely path of U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 Investors' worries about the U.S. current account deficit have been aggravated by concerns over the U.S. economy, which needs to attract a lot of foreign capital to plug the external shortfall.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 84.52 against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1240 GMT, having fallen on Thursday to 84.31, its lowest since 1995.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was little changed against the euro at $1.2860 , having fallen close to $1.2900 on Thursday, its lowest since February and within striking distance of record lows hit the same month.  \par \par \uc2 UNDER PRESSURE  \par \par \uc2 The dollar's recent decline has been broad-based with the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hitting 12-year lows against the Canadian dollar and its weakest in over 8 years against the Swiss franc.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar stood at 106.17 yen , having fallen below 105.70 on Thursday for the first time since April.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is focusing on the need for a correction in the U.S. current account deficit," said Adrian Schmidt, market strategist at Royal Bank of Scotland Financial Markets.  \par \par \uc2 "After several months of range-bound trading investors are keen to jump onto a trend."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters expect data at 1330 GMT to show the U.S. economy generated 169,000 new jobs in October, above the rise of 96,000 in September.  \par \par \uc2 A below-consensus number could be the catalyst for the dollar to hit record lows against the euro, although a figure above 200,000 could ignite a short-covering rally, since short-dollar positions have built up to unusually high levels.  \par \par \uc2 "Even if payrolls are in line with expectations, a test of record levels is possible," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels. "We are in a hugely dollar sceptical environment and it would take a lot to get a counter-trend."  \par \par \uc2 POLICY TOLERANCE  \par \par \uc2 Investors are keen to gauge how tolerant other countries will be of a further slide in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 French President Jacques Chirac said on Friday he was concerned by the fall in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding Europe would need to consider the repercussions on the region's growth and export prospects.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement said European policymakers were monitoring the euro's recent rise and Group of Seven finance ministers would discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues if the move continued. However, he sounded relaxed about the euro's current levels, saying he expected Germany's economy to cope.  \par \par \uc2 Austria's chancellor weighed in saying the euro's strength against the dollar provided no joy to euro zone exporters but had benefits in the form of cushioning the impact of high oil prices. Oil is priced in dollars.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet commented that fast \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves hurt economic growth, a well-worn refrain that analysts said did not suggest any great concern over the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, a senior politician said the yen's rise against the dollar had been rapid but there was no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market yet.  \par \par \uc2 Kaoru Yosano, policy chief of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, also told Reuters he did not expect Washington to aggressively pursue a weaker dollar as this would prompt capital outflows from the United States.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041105e0b500ewc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
