\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar mired near lows before U.S. jobs data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 678 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2004\par 12:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Nov 5 (Reuters) - The dollar teetered just above nine-year lows on a trade-weighted basis on Friday as investors waited for key U.S. jobs data before deciding whether to extend the greenback's recent decline.  \par \par \uc2 After some respite at the start of the week, the dollar came under heavy fire on Thursday as dealers turned attention away from the U.S. election and back to the United States' budget and current account deficits.  \par \par \uc2 The victory of U.S. President George W. Bush this week means four more years for his administration and most analysts do not foresee policy changes that would shrink deficits anytime soon.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is buying into the view that the U.S. may be happy with a weaker dollar to help correct its economic imbalances," said Paul Mackel, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ABN AMRO.  \par \par \uc2 "We are seeing a pause before the payrolls but generally I think people remain happy to play the trend and drive the dollar lower."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar traded at 84.47 against a basket of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 1120 GMT, having fallen on Thursday to 84.31, its lowest since 1995.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was little changed against the euro at $1.2870, having fallen close to $1.2900 on Thursday, its lowest since February and within striking distance of record lows hit that month.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback was also steady against the yen, at 106.10, having fallen on Thursday to its lowest against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  since mid-April.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. DATA WATCH  \par \par \uc2 Investors' worries about the U.S. current account deficit have been aggravated by concerns over the U.S. economy, which needs to attract a lot of foreign capital to plug the external shortfall.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. jobs data at 1330 GMT will be closely watched for clues on the sustainability of the U.S. recovery and the likely path of U.S. interest rates.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think we will attack the record (euro/dollar) highs before the payrolls data," said Peter Fontaine, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at KBC in Brussels.  \par \par \uc2 "But even if payrolls are in line with expectations, a test of record levels is possible. We are in a hugely dollar sceptical environment and it would take a lot to get a counter-trend."  \par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters expect the U.S. economy to have generated 169,000 new jobs in October, above the rise of 96,000 in September.  \par \par \uc2 A below-consensus number could be the catalyst for new dollar lows, although a figure above 200,000 could ignite a short-covering rally, since short-dollar positions have built up to unusually high levels.  \par \par \uc2 POLICY TOLERANCE  \par \par \uc2 Investors are keen to gauge how tolerant other countries will be of a further slide in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 French President Jacques Chirac said on Friday he was concerned by the fall in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , adding Europe would need to consider the repercussions on the region's growth and export prospects.  \par \par \uc2 German Economy Minister Wolfgang Clement said European policymakers were monitoring the euro's recent rise and Group of Seven finance ministers would discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues if the move continued. However, he sounded relaxed about the euro's current levels, saying he expected Germany's economy to cope.  \par \par \uc2 "Europe is concerned with inflation and a strong euro helps fight inflation," said ABN's Mackel. "At the moment, euro zone officials don't seem particularly worried."  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday, European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet commented that fast \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves hurt economic growth, a well-worn refrain that analysts said did not suggest any great concern over the euro's rise.  \par \par \uc2 In Japan, a senior politician said the yen's rise against the dollar had been rapid but there was no need for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market yet.  \par \par \uc2 Kaoru Yosano, policy chief of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, also told Reuters he did not expect Washington to aggressively pursue a weaker dollar as this would prompt capital outflows from the United States.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041105e0b500dnr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
