\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1-No need yet for  \highlight22\uc2 Japan fx intervention \highlight\uc2 -LDP official.  \b0\par\par\uc2 396 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 November 2004\par 10:19\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 5 (Reuters) - Although the yen's recent rise has been rapid, there is no need yet for Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, Kaoru Yosano, policy chief of the ruling Liberal Democratic Party, said on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 "It is a rapid move," Yosano told Reuters in an interview when asked about the dollar's fall by 5 yen over the past month.  \par \par \uc2 But asked if the movement had been enough to warrant \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he replied: "Not enough for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is determined by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Earlier on Friday Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said he was closely watching \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  levels, although he declined to say what an appropriate level for the yen was.  \par \par \uc2 Tanigaki repeated the government's standard position that the ministry would take appropriate action if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements deviate from economic fundamentals.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese authorities spent a record 20 trillion yen ($189 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last year and another 15 trillion yen from January to March in an effort to curb the yen's rise and have repeatedly said excessive and speculative moves in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market are undesirable.  \par \par \uc2 Yosano said he did not expect the United States to aggressively pursue a weaker dollar as some strength in the dollar was necessary for the U.S. stock and bond markets.  \par \par \uc2 "If the United States supports a weak dollar, that would prompt capital to flow out (from the United States) to Europe, so I think the U.S. authorities will remain cautious and will not voice their support for a weak dollar," Yosano said.  \par \par \uc2 If Washington does not aggressively pursue a weaker dollar, then there should not be a rapid fluctuation in dollar/yen rates, Yosano said. For that reason, he added: "They (Japanese authorities) may not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market for a while."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was hovering around 106 yen on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been under pressure due to worries about the strength of the U.S. economy and a gaping trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 On Thursday it fell to a seven-month low of 105.68 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Yosano said he did not expect the U.S economy to slow down sharply under the administration of President George W. Bush, and that the Japanese economy would continue growing gradually as a result.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041105e0b500anz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
