\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar weak after vote, economy back in focus.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Brent Kininmont  \par \uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2004\par 08:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 4 (Reuters) - The dollar hit an 8-= month low against the euro on Thursday after the spotlight quickly swung back to the U.S. economy and the growing U.S. trade gap once the presidential election was out of the way.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar came under pressure due to speculation that the administration of President George W. Bush would tolerate a weaker dollar to help narrow the trade deficit.  \par \par \uc2 "The U.S. hasn't got any funding problems now, but maybe there will be problems down the road if the current account deficit continues to grow without check," said Jake Moore, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Bank in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "And the check to that is that the dollar should decline."  \par \par \uc2 The shortfall in the current account, which measures trade and fund flows, hit a record $166.18 billion in the second quarter.  \par \par \uc2 The euro rose as far as $1.2847, its highest level since mid-February and up from $1.2820 in late U.S. trade. At 0655 GMT, it was at around $1.2830.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was around 106.25 yen after hitting a high around 106.90 overnight when it became clear that Bush had won the election. It soon lost those gains, falling to around 106.20 in late New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a 6-= month low of 105.77 yen earlier in the week as traders closed positions ahead of the vote.  \par \par \uc2 Bush won re-election to a second four-year term, beating Democratic Senator John Kerry. A close race had heightened fears that the election could drag on and be decided in the courts, as in 2000.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts had expected the dollar to benefit from a near-term bounce in the event of a swift, decisive result but it proved even swifter and shallower than most traders had anticipated.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was little changed at 136.30 yen  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  IN SIGHT?  \par \par \uc2 Analysts see the yen benefiting from the dollar's vulnerability, depending on oil and stock prices.  \par \par \uc2 "The yen is likely to pass the 105 level (by year-end)," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 "The only thing standing in its way at the moment is the stock market. If stock markets are unable to stage a really conclusive rally amid higher oil prices, then the yen might ... stay range-bound."  \par \par \uc2 But the market is also nervous that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stem the yen's rise - and protect an export-led economic recovery - should the dollar drop quickly below 105 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Some traders say the next trigger for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be around 103.40 yen, a four-year low for the dollar hit on March 31.  \par \par \uc2 "Japan is less sensitive to the 105/106 levels than it was early this year because companies are reasonably comfortable with the current level," said Moore of Barclays.  \par \par \uc2 The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a record 20 trillion yen ($188.5 billion) during 2003 and another 14.83 trillion yen in the first three months of this year to hold down the yen. It has not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei stock average ended up 0.54 percent on Thursday but failed to hold above 11,000.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil futures traded at around $50.52 per barrel, some way below the record high above $55 reached late last month.  \par \par \uc2 With the election over, the market is expected to focus on economic indicators for clues about the health of the U.S. economy, particularly employment data due on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 A Reuters poll showed U.S. non-farm payrolls rising by 169,000 in October, compared with a rise of 96,000 in September.  \par \par \uc2 Despite widespread expectations that a significant number of new jobs were added in October, some analysts are concerned that forecasts are too high and that the dollar could be thumped lower if the figure is disappointing. ($1=106.09 yen) (Additional reporting by Naomi Tajitsu and Dan Sloan).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020041104e0b4004vm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
