\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Little Changed Vs Majors After US Vote  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Stanley White  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 867 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2004\par 06:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar was flat against the yen and the euro in Asia Thursday as investors saw little reason to take aggressive positions just after U.S. president George W. Bush was elected for a second term.  \par \par \uc2 With many market watchers conceding that the Republican's re-election will lead to very little change in economic or foreign policy, investors instead chose to return their attention to U.S. economic fundamentals, including the current account and budget deficits and the economic data calendar.  \par \par \uc2 "There are still concerns about the double deficits," said Naomi Fink, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at BNP Paribas. "But now that both houses of Congress are also dominated by Republicans, from a budget perspective we're not likely to see huge improvements."  \par \par \uc2 In federal elections held Tuesday, Bush's Republican party expanded its majority in both the upper and lower legislative houses. Many market participants also agree that because of this increased advantage, the Republicans are unlikely to trim the country's current account deficit either.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. federal government racked up a $413 billion budget deficit in fiscal year 2004, which is about 3.6% of gross domestic product and a record in dollar terms. In the first eight months of this year, the U.S. current account deficit rose to $426 billion, or almost 6% of GDP.  \par \par \uc2 In the weeks leading up to the presidential vote, investors had sold the dollar on worries that U.S. government spending was out of control and that the country relied too heavily on overseas capital to fund its growth. While some investors may choose to sell the greenback now that the election is over, other market participants say so much negative news has already been priced into the dollar that it may be difficult to push it much lower against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 U.S. non-farm payrolls data for October due Friday are expected to have added 200,000 new jobs, according to a survey of economists by Dow Jones Newswires. If the report meets expectations or is slightly better, the dollar may not move that much, some traders say.  \par \par \uc2 However, if the numbers disappoint, the dollar could fall to Y105.00, while the euro could rise to $1.30, a new all-time high, according to Fink at BNP Paribas.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Rates At 00:50 EDT / 0450 GMT \par  \par                          Latest     Previous   %Chg   Daily   Daily    %Chg \par Dollar Rates                        2150 GMT          High \par    Low      12/31 \par  \par USD/JPY Yen              106.21-26  106.21-26   0.00  106.29  106.01   -1.05 \par EUR/USD Euro             1.2819-23  1.2819-23   0.00  1.2830  1.2814   +1.77 \par GBP/USD Sterling         1.8474-78  1.8489-95  -0.08  1.8498  1.8472   +3.42 \par USD/CHF Swiss Franc      1.1935-41  1.1930-36  +0.04  1.1947  1.1928   -3.72 \par USD/CAD Canadian Dlr     1.2053-61  1.2084-89  -0.26  1.2092  1.2053   -7.23 \par AUD/USD Australian Dlr   0.7559-64  0.7562-64  -0.04  0.7573  0.7554   +0.36 \par NZD/USD New Zealand Dlr  0.6877-84  0.6902-12  -0.36  0.6912  0.6882   +4.99 \par USD/DEM Deutsche Mark    2.2150-75  2.2150-75   0.00  2.2157  2.2160   -0.11 \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 04-11-04 0518GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another factor that may limit the dollar's downside against the yen is fear of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken the Japanese unit.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think we should rule out \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as we've seen a large build up in speculative yen-long positions," BNP Paribas' Fink also said. According to data from the Chicago Mercantile Exchange, net yen-long positions stood at 27,636 during the week to Oct. 26, more than triple the amount from the previous week. If these positions grow even more, the MOF may have to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market to prevent wild swings, Fink added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will also focus on a Federal Reserve monetary policy meeting next week on Wednesday. While most investors expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to raise interest rates by 25 basis points to 2.00%, any signs about the future pace of interest rate hikes could be used as a reason to buy or sell the dollar, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, yields for benchmark 10-year U.S. government debt may be even more important for the dollar than policy moves from the Fed.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "If (10-year treasury) yields rise above 4.2%, then sentiment for the dollar would change," said Osamu Takashima, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Bank of Tokyo Mitsubishi. Higher yields would make dollar-denominated assets, and therefore the dollar, more popular with investors, Takashima added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year Treasury note stood at 101 10/32, down 3/32 to yield 4.085% in Asian trading, slightly above the 4.07% yield at the end of trading in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By Stanley White, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; stanley.white@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Bennett Richardson [ 04-11-04 0629GMT ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020041104e0b40004e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
