\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Strong Dollar Not A Policy But A Relic  \b0\par\par\uc2 891 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2004\par 21:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Robert Flint \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Don't expect an announcement in Yankee Stadium to the accompaniment of brass bands, but the U.S. strong dollar policy will fade into total obscurity over the next few years.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 The Secretary of the Treasury, whether John Snow or someone else, may continue to pay lip service to a strong dollar for a while, but that's simply a precaution to prevent a disorderly plunge of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Snow's most recent avowals of a strong dollar policy sound more like a cheerleader rooting for a losing team than a recitation of the mantra made famous by former Treasury Secretary Robert Rubin.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, a public renunciation of the dollar policy that's been in place for nearly ten years would amount to an invitation to sell the greenback, sort of like printing "50% off" on every dollar bill.  \par \par \uc2 That's why we'll never see the sharp-break scenario mentioned by Snow's predecessor at Treasury, Paul O'Neill. In an interview in February, 2001, O'Neill said markets would know if the U.S. ever shifted its dollar policy because he would hire New York's Yankee Stadium and a "bunch of brass bands" to make the announcement.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, the Bush administration will allow the entire concept to slip into obscurity. Since January 2000, no one has believed that top U.S. economic officials are distressed by an orderly decline for the dollar. The strong dollar has become a relic of the past, not an indication of current policy. Measured by almost any standard, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has to depreciate from unsustainable levels both to relieve competitive pressure on U.S. manufacturers and to help correct payment imbalances.  \par \par \uc2 However, there's an extremely fine line between a desirable downward slide and a full-scale crash. The biggest risks lie in the enormous momentum that builds at lightning speed in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, as well as the tendency for exchange rates to overshoot. That's why central bankers around the globe have emphasized that the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements - not a particular level - is the most worrying factor.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Strong Dollar A Repudiation Of Earlier Policy \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 During the heyday of international \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management in the 1980's, the Plaza and Louvre accords, while initially successful, may have started an avalanche in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets that eventually spilled over into the U.S. stock market as the crash of 1987.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The formulation of the strong dollar policy under Rubin was essentially a repudiation of attempts to manage \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates on a grand scale. Rubin bumped the determination of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  values from conferences among leading nations to trading floors. Markets, not heads of states or finance ministers, would decide a fair value for the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Moreover, U.S. officials would no longer try to influence the dollar's rate through jawboning. As a tactical move in a larger game, some of Rubin's predecessors had deliberately "talked" the dollar in one direction or the other. Rubin limited his comments to one sentence - A strong dollar is the national interest - that quickly became known as the strong dollar mantra.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At no time did Rubin give any hint at what levels the dollar was considered strong or weak. To him, the source of the dollar's strength was its attractiveness as an instrument for investment, not its relative value on any given day.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rubin wasn't a fan of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , both for theoretical as well as practical reasons. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to influence wasn't sound policy, nor did it seem to work very well. However, on a few occasions, Treasury Secretaries in the Clinton administration backed concerted \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to prevent disorderly movements in markets, perhaps mostly as a nod to counterparts in Europe and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bush administration has been equally cool to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a means of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  management, although it has cut Japan a lot of slack in slowing the yen's appreciation. What we may see, however, is a return to top-level international arrangements a la Plaza or Louvre to determine \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  trends, if not levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the Group of Seven leading economies plus China can hammer out a plan to assure an orderly long-term depreciation of the dollar, the U.S. may well play along. That, far more than the greenback slipping under Y100 or the euro popping above $1.35, would sound the strong dollar's death knell. Given the effect the quasi yuan peg has in limiting the dollar's broad depreciation against a basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now, China's involvement would be key to the success of any such process.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A return to coordinated international action on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rates could prove the best option. The unilateral declaration of a strong dollar policy served U.S. interests during the Clinton administration, but different times and challenges require a different policy. As a relic, the strong dollar policy doesn't have much power.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Robert Flint, an assistant news editor, has covered \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires for the past seven years and was previously Beijing bureau chief and chief correspondent in Scandinavia.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Robert Flint; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4408; robert.flint@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 11-04-04 1543ET ]  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041104e0b400031\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
