\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Dollar Resumes Selling Trend As Election Seems Certain-4-  \b0\par\par\uc2 326 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2004\par 16:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2004, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 The euro and sterling were helped Wednesday by better than expected services Purchasing Management Index in the United Kingdom and the Euro zone. That comes ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next meeting Thursday. The bank is not expected to change rates.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was not up against the dollar, partly because markets in Tokyo were shut overnight for a holiday. In addition, Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital, said traders are reluctant to bet too much on a yen rise, because of continued fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 'There's just some concern in the market that the BOJ will find ... (a further yen climb) worrisome,' Englander said.  \par \par \uc2 Englander noted that while Japanese officials have gradually ratcheted up their verbal concerns about the yen's rise, European officials continue to sound almost upbeat about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening, saying it helps Euro zone countries deal with high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Australian dollar climbed to $0.7556, from $0.7468 late Tuesday, despite the Reserve Bank of Australia's decision to keep rates unchanged at 5.25%. Disappointing overnight retail and trade data also failed to keep down the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is now at fresh six and a half month highs.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates on hold at 1.75% Wednesday as expected, citing low inflation. With a monetary policy inflation target of 2.5%, the bank said, 'At present, inflation is considerably lower than this.'  \par \par \uc2 Norway is recovering from a period of weak growth from 2002 to 2003. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held rates at a historical low of 1.75% since March, in an effort to spur economic growth and boost inflation.  \par \par \uc2 (Ian Talley in Oslo contributed to this article.)  \par \par \uc2 -Laurence Norman; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -0-   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020041103e0b300bkr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
