\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Resumes Selling Trend As Election Seems Certain  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Laurence Norman  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 828 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 November 2004\par 14:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2004 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK [Dow Jones]--After a brief overnight rally, the dollar is lower against most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in early New York trading Wednesday, with analysts saying the market had already shifted its focus away from the U.S. elections and back to economic concerns.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  had climbed against the euro and the yen in overnight trading as it became increasingly likely that the presidential vote would hand President George W. Bush a second term. The result has not yet been confirmed.  \par \par \uc2 However, the dollar fell back rapidly going into New York trading, with the election bounce was pretty much over, according to traders.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the dollar right now is weakening for a lot of the same reasons it was before the election. We are in the same world we were in before the election, which was a dollar negative landscape," said Lara Rhame, Vice President Global \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at CSFB. "I think the market has already priced in a Bush victory."  \par \par \uc2 Early in New York, the euro is at $1.2798 from an overnight low of $1.2657 and $1.2725 late Tuesday. The dollar is at Y106.21, up slightly from Y106.10 late Tuesday and at CHF1.1958 from CHF1.2038. Sterling is at $1.8493 from $1.8394.  \par \par \uc2 The main focus Wednesday will be on any concession in the presidential race and the oil inventory numbers that will be released during the morning by the Department of Energy. [ 11-03-04 0854ET ]  \par \par \uc2 The Institute for Supply Management survey of the U.S. non-manufacturing sector for September will also be released Wednesday morning, while traders say there is already fresh short-dollar positioning ahead of Friday's non-farm payrolls data.  \par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the election and politics will not be completely out the picture Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Rhame said the dollar could get "a tiny dollar bounce" if the election result is made official during Wednesday trading, but that any rise would be quickly ceded in profit taking.  \par \par \uc2 On the other side, she warned that the dollar could face even more downward pressure if there is no definitive election result by the weekend.  \par \par \uc2 "I think it matters. Everybody is sort of hoping that it's over today (Wednesday). If we get near the weekend and there's no result ... I think that would be even more dollar negative," said Rhame.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in a research report Wednesday, analysts at UBS said that - providing Bush is called as the winner - the market could react to any reshuffle in the foreign policy and national security teams, while "there will also be a focus on any signals that the White House and the Congressional leadership are poised to move towards fiscal consolidation," the report said.  \par \par \uc2 The Republicans widened their majority in both the House of Representatives and the Senate, making it easier for Bush to make his tax cuts permanent, if he gets the second term. [ 11-03-04 0904ET ]  \par \par \uc2 The euro and sterling were helped Wednesday by better than expected services Purchasing Management Index in the United Kingdom and the Euro zone. That comes ahead of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next meeting Thursday. The bank is not expected to change rates.  \par \par \uc2 The yen was not up against the dollar, partly because markets in Tokyo were shut overnight for a holiday. In addition, Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital, said traders are reluctant to bet too much on a yen rise, because of continued fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "There's just some concern in the market that the BOJ will find ... (a further yen climb) worrisome," Englander said.  \par \par \uc2 Englander noted that while Japanese officials have gradually ratcheted up their verbal concerns about the yen's rise, European officials continue to sound almost upbeat about the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strengthening, saying it helps Euro zone countries deal with high oil prices.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Australian dollar climbed to $0.7556, from $0.7468 late Tuesday, despite the Reserve Bank of Australia's decision to keep rates unchanged at 5.25%. Disappointing overnight retail and trade data also failed to keep down the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is now at fresh six and a half month highs.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Norwegian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept interest rates on hold at 1.75% Wednesday as expected, citing low inflation. With a monetary policy inflation target of 2.5%, the bank said, "At present, inflation is considerably lower than this."  \par \par \uc2 Norway is recovering from a period of weak growth from 2002 to 2003. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has held rates at a historical low of 1.75% since March, in an effort to spur economic growth and boost inflation.  \par \par \uc2 (Ian Talley in Oslo contributed to this article.)  \par \par \uc2 -Laurence Norman; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; laurence.norman@dowjones.com [ 11-03-04 0950ET ]  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020041103e0b300016\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw18